The parliament did not support an amendment liberating Tymoshenko
The Ukrainian parliament today dismissed the proposal as part of an amendment to the penal code, on the basis of which Opposition leader Yulia Tymoshenko was sentenced.
An amendment that would pave the way for the liberation of imprisoned ex-premier was dismissed at the second reading of a proposal to commute sentences for economic offenses.
Tymoshenko was sentenced to seven years imprisonment in October for concluding an alleged unfavourable treaty with Russia to buy natural gas.
The verdict is not final, the convict's appellate is to be dealt with by the court in December.
Tymoshenko denounces the verdict as a political vengeance of the regime, suspicion of biattenuating the trial provoked a process even in the West.
The proposal to fall out of the Criminal Code of Section 365, on the basis of which the ex-premier was convicted, was supported by 147 deputies.
226 votes were needed for its approval.
