The crosshairs of investors have France and the remainder of Europe come into the crosshairs
Statistical whirlwind swept across Europe on Tuesday.
Individual states have published data on GDP for 3Q.
The Czech economy stagnated quarter-on-quarter, Germany and France are growing.
However, the bond market is tearing up records today.
Investors don't just look at Italy, but also Spain, France, Austria and others.
Statistical authorities of the euro area, Germany, the Czech Republic and some other European countries have published preliminary estimates for the development of the economy for the third quarter.
Germany's GDP grew by an expected 0.5%, France by 0.4%.
At the same time, the two countries were modifying the data for the second quarter, in which Germany was better than expected (growing by 0.3% instead of the original 0.1%), but France said it emerged in a slight decrease of 0.1% (the original figures talked about stagnation).
The Czech economy grew by 1.5 percent year-on-year in the third quarter this year.
However quarter-on-quarter the gross domestic product (GDP) stagnated and, according to the Czech Statistical Office, confirmed the trend of a gradual slowdown in the growth of the economy’s performance.
Respondents are inferior to analyst estimates.
The most important events:
The Czech economy grew by 1.5 per cent year-on-year in the third quarter this year, but stagnated quarter-on-quarter.
The German economy grew by 0.5 per cent in the third quarter according to pre-season-recommended and sequential figures.
The economy of both the euro area and the European Union increased by 0.2 percent compared to the previous quarter compared to the previous quarter, in line with the expectations of analysts.
The Greek economy fell 5.2% year-on-year.
Proceedings on her 10-year-old bond is 28.45%.
Proceedings of Italian and Spanish bonds are rising towards critical borders.
Austria, the Netherlands, France, Belgium, etc
Revenues for Czech 10-year-old bonds remain below 4%, even if the situation in the region is deteriorating.
From Germany, there are votes that the ECB should be the lender of last resort.
The Czechs qualified for the euro.
