How tour prices have evolved since 1989
Until 1989, only five state-owned travel agencies were allowed in what was then Czechoslovakia: Čedok, Sporturist, CKM, Rekrea and Autoturist.
At that time, travel agencies overwhelmingly sold only trips to the countries of the socialist bloc, because travel to the west was very limited.
Czech hikers thus traveled on holiday to the sea to the GDR (Norwegian Democratic Republic of Germany), to the Soviet Union (Sochi in Crimea by the Black Sea), to Bulgaria, Romania (both to the Black Sea) and Hungary (Balaton).
A clear exception was the period from 1962 to 1972, when it was also possible to travel to Yugoslavia, then the Yugoslav regime leaned towards the West and the journey of Czech tourists to Yugoslavia was subsequently very limited.
The great paradox of the time of socialism was that the citizens of the Socialist bloc (i.e. also Czechoslovaks) were not allowed to travel freely even within the socialist countries.
In the event that a citizen of Czechoslovakia wanted to travel individually, for example, to Poland or to the Soviet Union, so he needed an invitation letter from this country, otherwise an individual journey was not possible.
At that time, travel agencies also offered trips to the West, but it was necessary to queue overnight with a sleeping bag or have "good aquaintances" in the travel agency.
However, it has not yet been won.
The so-called foreign exchange promise, which was a precondition for trips to the West, was not awarded to "politically unreliable people", who could not travel at all.
The Czechoslovak currency was not a freely exchangerable currency, so it was not possible to raise funds (Western currency) for a journey to the West.
Near most often, however, the Czechs spent a holiday in the Czech Republic, which resulted in a large number of chalets and cottages that the Czechs bought and this custom survives to this day.
The journey of the Czechs has thus experienced a real revolution since 1989.
While until 1989 holidays in the GDR or Bulgaria were our maximum, last year Czechs spent 4.5 million holidays abroad in countries all over the world.
A huge paradox, which we do not already realize today, is the fact that it has not been possible to travel freely for us for the currently most-popular Croatia until 1989, because it was a part of Yugoslavia at that time.
Immediately at the beginning of the 90's of the last century there was abolition of the visa waiver into neighboring Western European countries, which kick-started a huge wave of short-term visits mainly to Austrian Vienna or Germany.
For a long time the Czechoslovaks couldn't get enough trips to the West, everyone just wanted to go there for at least for a while.
In Paris, for example, tours of Czechs slept in parks in tents, because our income at that time did not allow us to be able to pay a hotel or a guesthouse.
A typical feature of the travel of the Czechs at this time was carving their own food, cans, sausages, etc. in order to save money.
Another important moment was the introduction of a freely convertible Czech koruna, so our hikers were finally able to freely buy the Western currency after many daisies.
However, our standard of living, as well as average salaries increased, which resulted in another important moment around 1997.
Thanks to the increased demand for travel, travel agencies have regularly started to prepare long-leaf-fashioned flights since 1997, making air trips incredibly cheaper thanks to the opportunity to negotiate quantitative discounts compared to the past.
At the end of the 90's of the last century, the Czechs already had vacuant traffic to virtually all developed countries of the world, apart from the U.S., which further facilitated our travel.
Another interesting moment in the history of post-revolutionary travel came just in 2000, when, thanks to a considerable demand, it was dispatched from the Czech Republic historically the first direct charter flight into exotics, it was to Thailand.
Starting this year from this year, direct charters with Czech tourists flies to warm far-fledged countries, this winter it will fly directly from the Czech Republic to twelve exotic destinations.
Exotic countries are currently visited by about 100 thousand Czechs a year!
Another interesting moment in this period, which symbolizes our post-revolutionary development, came in 2008.
On November 17, 2008, the U.S. abolished visas for Czech tourists and thus our country definitely ranked among the most developed countries in the world.
In 2008, the Czechs also spent more than half of their holidays abroad (50.3%) for the first time in its history and for the first time in history they spent more than CZK 15 thousand per person for the first time in their history, including the cost of their stay.
Pile is supposed to find the chairman, but there is a risk of stalemate again
For almost five months there is Czech football without a chairman and there is a risk that Ivan Hašek's successor at the head of the Football Association of the Czech Republic (FAČR) will not be known even after another extraordinary general meeting that will take place in Nymbourka on Thursday.
There is still no agreement according to what electoral rules will take place.
Without it, it will not even start to vote on which of the three candidates to be elected.
Jindřich Rajchl, Miroslav Pelta and Tomáš Paclík have an interest in leading Czech football.
Hašek resigned in June and on his departure called on his colleagues to unify and find a strong personality to lead football in the future.
But in an era after Hašek, football swept back into squabbles between the two union chambers, which can not coincides on a common candidate.
It turned out full on September 16, when a new chairman was to be chosen.
Delegates of the General Meeting flocked unnecessarily to the National House in Prague-Smíchov, because the negotiations remained blocked and it wasn’t just the method of election.
There was also no agreement on who has the right to actually vote on the meeting - whether only statutory representatives, or even the functionaries entrusted with a power of attorney.
There has been a partial consensus on this single point since then, which was also helped by the recommendations of the Institute for the State and the law, according to which representation on the basis of a power of attorney is not possible.
Disputes still prevail over the electoral order.
Even the Executive Committee did not bring much clarity into the situation, which already solves basically nothing else at its meetings, whether proper or extraordinary.
The view of the possibility of choice has changed several times, but the neuralgic point is still the same.
The question is: how to proceed in Round 3 in order to exclude the possibility of a heel?
"The distrophy is whether to proceed in accordance with the statutes or to find a different model," Dušan Svoboda, who represents professional football in the leadership of the association, said.
According to the statute-advised model in the third round, the candidate will win, which will have a greater percentage support in the chamber for which he is running.
Morava is reportedly willing to go on a concession in order to win a candidate who will receive a higher sum of a percentage of the votes from both chambers.
And the Czech side promotes the condition that the winner must gain at least a two-three supremacy at this percentage census.
"But then it would lose the meaning of the two previous rounds, it would completely deny the two-chamber concept," Svoboda understands the objections of the Moravian Freedom party, according to which it would be best not to bend the order and to vote according to the applicable statutes.
Tomáš Paclík also somewhat surprisingly agrees, although it is estimating that the statute-determined Electoral Code most suits Pelta among all candidates.
"I wish to be chosen according to the statutes as far as possible."
"In order not to think of some representative nonsensical nonsense as at the last general meeting," said the owner Viktoria of Pilsen, who is running for the first time and in his comments defined himself primarily against Pelta.
Pelta remains annoying.
In Nymburka, according to him, the new chairman will be known and he believes that the candidates will cooperate constructively with each other in the future.
"All your passions will be reassuminated by today's progress of the national team at the European Championships."
"Football will again be on a happy wave, there will be a greater willingness to work together," says the Jablonec official, who has the greatest experience in managing football at both the professional and regional level.
Rajchl is more restrained.
"I'm hoping for a deal, but unfortunately the situation's a lot apart."
"It is not among the people from Bohemia and Moravia, but there are attempts to adapt the regulations on purpose."
"This can cause the blocking of the general meeting to happen again."
"However, I hope that common sense prevails," Rajchl said.
There is not much time left for the agreement of all parties.
Some negotiations were supposed to take place directly in Montenegro, but who knows if the football generality will be complete for the match at all.
An aeronautical special, in which they were supposed to travel to Podgorica, among others, vice-chairmen Dalibor Kučera and Rajchl, did not fly away from Prague due to a technical disorder in the morning and a replacement option was searched for how to transport a part of the executive committee to a barrage retaliation.
In the end, a ministerial-greiter-level meeting may be decisive, which may occur allegedly on Wednesday.
The Minister of Education, Youth and Sports Josef Dobeš would like to force divided parties within the strongest sports association in the country to reach an agreement.
There is a lot in the game: state subsidy, trustworthiness of football and the forthfinished general meeting of ČSTV, which will deal with the new direction of Czech sport after Sazka's financial problems.
Paclík goes into the fight for the chairman because he does not see a suitable nominee
If Tomáš Paclík, the head of the footballers of Pilsen Tomáš Paclík, before an extraordinary general meeting, saw a strong nominee who would meet all his ideas about the new chairman of the Football Association of the Czech Republic (FAČR), he would not even apply to the fight for the top position.
"Should a high-quality adept exist, I'm fighting for it and I don't go into an election."
"But in the current situation, no one has been able to find it like this," Paclík told the Czech News Agency.
Until the duel with Miroslav Pelta and Jindřich Rajchl, Paclík’s motivation to try to prevent Pelta, the alleged favorite of the elections, from trying to prevent him from going to the head of the FAČR.
Paclík also emphasizes the fact that, in the event of his victory as chairman, he would last only until 2013, when the general meeting is on the agenda.
He would like to see an experienced and top-of-the-line manager who isn't very associated with football.
"I've been talking to some people who would come into consideration."
"For me it was crazy when some of them said they didn't even want to identify with Czech football at the moment."
"I'd like to change such a perspective," he said.
Paclík is now most bothered by Pelta's connection with the chairman of the Pilsen Regional Football Association Roman Berbre, a man with a controversial reputation, who is known backstage as a mighty lobbyist with the ability to influencing the voting of delegates at the general meeting.
It is so impassable for Paclík that Pelta should stand in a presiding position.
"If he had won, the current situation would be preserved and nothing would have moved forward in football."
"In combination with Berbro's support, who is a proficient businesswoman, it wouldn't benefit football even more," Paclík said.
It also alludes that Pelta's name years ago was heard in the wiretaps in the investigation of the corruption affair, from which Pelta went unpunished unpunished.
"I don't know what prestige Pelto's victory would bring."
"And what we would also look like in the eyes of UEFA and FIFA," said Paclík, who is already taking his former statement that he would "sell the Pilsen club if Pelta became chairman".
In the event that Paclík triumphed in Thursday's election, he would be able to conceive of future cooperation with Pelta.
Another candidate Rajchl, whom Paclík supported before the September general meeting, which ended in a fiasco.
"He (Pelta) would be the first I'd go after."
"He has great powers."
"I'd be able to think of him as the head of representation."
"But he wouldn't be able to run an association managerially."
"I also don't stick my nose where I don't know," said Paclík, who attracted a lot of attention in September by defending the referees commission headed by Luděk Macela, who was replaced by Dagmar Damková.
Paclík claims that he would dare the management of the association.
Reliance on its skills from the management of private companies, since last summer it has also been the head of Pilsen, which with his support celebrated the premier's league triumph in May.
"I've got the strength to make a difference in a football environment, to contribute to a certain cultivation."
"In order to limit the influences of some interest groups, which may have a tendency to influencing the Disciplinary and Expection Commission, which goes into distasteful situations," said Paclík, who does not hide his antipathy, for example, for the Spartan boss Daniel Křetínský.
As one of the priorities he would also consider the strengthening of relations of the FAČR with the Czech Olympic Committee, with the help of which he lobbied for political leaders to get more money from the lottery tax into the sport.
"It's one of the key things the new president should take care of."
"Whether there will be a tax on gamblers, as the political parties declutter, I understand their argument, but frankly they have to say where the money will go," he said.
As a burden, Paclík now does not even take his connection to the Pilsen club, even though he himself argues that the chairman of the FAČR should be "residential".
Pelta in turn is associated with Jablonec, Rajchl and Dukla in Prague.
"It's not ideal."
"But I don't even see the point in mishandling my shares at a Pilsen club and returning there in a year and a year and a year and a.m," Paclík said.
Pelta Promises New Statutes of the FAČR that would expel electoral heel
Two days before the General Meeting of the Football Association, one of the nominees for chairman, Miroslav Pelta, vowed to be enforced if he was elected so that the FAČR has new statutes, which in the future would definitively exterminate disputes over the electoral rules.
Jablonec's official is an optimist in Nymbourka ahead of Thursday's meeting in Nymbourka and believes that the pre-election fight will significantly dampn the eventual success of today's football team.
Pelta has already vouchanted an unrestrained choice before the unrealized election that he intends to concentrate on representing and raising funds if elected.
