The House of the 21st Century in Vienna
Moving pavilion
Ineborg Wiensowski
The story of architecture with peaks, falls and a happy ending: The Austrian pavilion from the World Exhibition in Brussels 1958 won prizes, became a museum in Vienna, remained vacured and desolated.
It is now reopened as an exhibition house.
When it is mountainous for modern post-war architecture, for bright forms, glass halls, courageous booms and new building materials that are typical for the whirlief in technical progress, the Austrian pavilion of architect Karel Schwanzer is an astonishing and famous example for the Brussels World Exhibition 1958.
Schwanzer's pavilion at that time received the Grand Prix d'Architecture for its light and high-performance steel-and-shoulder construction.
The building is still considered a milestone of contemporary architecture.
Famous the architect in such a way that during the next world exhibition he also built a pavilion of Austria.
With its legendary four-cylinder BMW-palace plus as the key carried out by a secondary structure in Munich, he eventually became internationally famous in 1973.
