But it wasn’t advisable for a museum building, neither spatially nor technically – there was a lack of walls for the suspension of artistic exhibits, there was no satisfactory climate.
Nevertheless, it has been in use until contemporary art has received a new tabernacle in Vienna.
Since 2001 the building has been vacant and decayed more and more.
Now architect Adolf Krischanitz has reconstructed the pavilion, it now bears the "House of the 21st Century" and will in the future be used as a Museum of Contemporary Art.
On Tuesday evening, the Belvedere Museum, to which the palace belongs, will open the first exhibition - a happy intent in every respect.
Agnes Husslein-Arco became the new director of the Austrian Belvedere Gallery in 2007.
Husslein-Arco, an promoted art historian with an efficiencilled career, among others in her youth figure skate-to-ebruary, later in Sotheby's, Guggenheim Musseum and founding director of Musea Moderna Salzburg, strongly promoted refurbishment among politicians and conservationists, acquiring public funds and private sponsorship money, and determined its use.
In Addition to contemporary art, the works of sculptor Fritz Wotruba should be exhibited with a foundation of the same name, there should be a café and a bookstore, and an office building had to be erected.
Strict oversight, low budget.
Krischanitz was an ideal occupnament.
It is not only that he sensitively reconnaissance re-reconstructed and rediscovered Joseph Olbrich’s Art Nouveau structures in Vienna and won the competition, but because he studied at Schwanzer at the Technical University of Vienna and the pavilion was an “extremely an important place for him” where he “alternately every weekend”.
He Knew the primordial project and he also knew the problems of the building, which, when relocated to Vimea and the change of use, it lost its generosity and frivolity.
There have been much bigger problems during the current reconstruction: the building was a "catautter for energy," Krischanitz says.
The profiles of the glass façade windows have been exchanged, used glass fiber-reinforced insulating insulating glass and a glass roof replaced by a special safety glass.
Refrigerated-steel girders have been strengthened or exchanged and asbestos-completed ceilings refurbished.
Two subterranean floors provided a place for new use, the recessed lighting duct between the building and the street is provided by illumination, a bridge leads to the entrance - almost a new building, but still a lominated Schwanzer pavilion.
The original entrance door has been retained, the building is painted in an old anti-corro-destroying reddish-brown color tone.
And the canoe is even exactly the same as it used to be.
And all this under the strict oversight of the heritage office and the low budget.
“Oftifully material had to be found that is good and inexpensive and he also liked the heritage office,” Krischanitz says, pointing to the shattered basement floor.
In spite of all the pressures, problems and refurbishments, the building remained flexible and aerial.
Difficulously, it is said to be with the pavilion "who Came here from Brussels", Krischanitz recently said a typical "Drop-in-the-City-history".
Such an architecture stands "always in the wrong place" and still wants to "buy proper self-confidence".
In this case, however, the House of the 21st century is not in an inappropriate place, because an urban happy accident has happened: A completely redesigned "Municipal Quarter at the Main Railway Station" with office and residential houses is being created around the Schweizer Garten.
And perhaps there will be another happy coincidence: The 21st-centicured house could receive support from another Krischanitz building, because the collector Francesca von Habsburg has bought the Berliner Temporäre Kunsthalle and, as it can be heard, he is already negotiating with the city about the neighborhood at the Schweizer Garten site.
