And on the other hand, as an investment bank advanced to the league of the world's largest banks
That impressed.
Hans Geiger, a former banker and later long-time researcher at Zurich, recounts: “I always cited UBS as a role model of a good bank to my students at the time.”
"From today's point of view, one asks: How could I just be mistaken?"
Waied greatly.
For the first time, it was obvious that something had gone wrong in UBS.
In the middle of the year, Peter Wuffli's head of the board retired, resolutely retired as a highly-intelligent banker.
A few months later, he wondered why he had left.
The bank's investment division squandered subprime-papers and had to write off billions.
In order to withstood this, UBS announced a capital increase of really 13 billion francs.
On the other hand, it actually found investors, but those over which it would recently turn its nose up: An opaque Asian state fund from Singapore and an Arab state-of-the-clock fund, which was so discrefeophistic that one didn't even learn its name.
But that's just the first crash.
It went worse in April 2008.
The bank had to award new billion-dollar losses, the president of the board of directors Marcel Osspel, 2006 the second highest-payed board of directors of Switzerland, withdrew.
Disastritis followed in October 2008: due to its erroneous investments under the financial crisis, UBS had to be rescued by the state.
The nationalbank has repurchased tens of billion-billion-dollar problematic securities from UBS, the Swiss Confederation put six-billion-dollar quenaires into compulsive bonds for which it could have become a collaborator of the bank.
The whole of Switzerland has been shaken - because it has noticed how dramatic it is for a small country when it has such large banks on its territory.
After all, the balance sheet of UBS 2007 was more than five times greater than the performance of the Swiss economy (see picture).
“We couldn’t salute UBS one more time,” Geiger said.
The first catastrophe follows the second.
A few months later, UBS capitulated in a tax dispute with America and expressed its willingness to release data from wealthy customers who, as far as possible, with the help of the banks cut taxes.
In addition, it has agreed to dispare $780 million - as a recurrence of unauthorised profits from these deals, including a fine mark-up.
In doing so, UBS has confessed more or less bluck-through that it has assisted cutting taxes.
At the same time, she has lost something that has always been her capital in competition for wealthy clients: a halo of discrepancy.
After this scandal, Swiss bank seceiod was no longer what they did before.
