UBS knows what the customer is looking for: security.
However, this idea of the Swiss-brown-grown big-gauge, UBS and Credit Suisse has sometimes been suppressed or betrayed, as the head of a small Swiss private bank finds out.
His name would rather not want to read in the newspapers, in the end he concludes, like all Swiss bankers, also with both large-scale deals.
UBS has probably gotten too shrouded, it finds - especially in investment banking, where the Anglo-Saxons have also expanded.
I don't think the Swedes have anything to say.
"And if they did, they've adapted to Anglo-Saxon ways a long time ago."
It really was the Anglo-Saxon investment banking that UBS switched, it brought high profits at first, and then a scoundrel: No European financial institute has lost as much money as Schweizer Großbank in the Subprime jungle.
On October 15, 2008, the catastrophe was obvious.
"On that Wednesday, the governing coalition of a small seven-million-strong country has decided on the largest bail-out event in the history of its nation," Lukas Hässig writes in his "Der UBS-Crash".
Up to $68 billion francs or around €45 billion have been approved by politicians to save a big-scale bankruptcy.
How did that happen?
Speaking with Swiss bankers, you see a problem especially in the size of UBS.
In June 1998, two of the three Swiss big banks merged into a mighty concern.
There was a bank, which already during the merger was too big for little Switzerland - and continued to grow.
At the same time, it facilitated the fact that the two different banking predecessors of UBS were complemented by: One wealthy but drowsy bank in Zurich with a center of gravity in the management of assets and poorer, but internationally more heavily involved.
Suddenly, inter-nationalized investment banknotors came advantageously for a lot of money, with which they could spew great roulette.
There are two names associated with UBS’s rise: Peter Wuffli, from 2001 to 2007, the head of UBS and Marcel Ospel, the chairman of the board of directors at that time.
“I’ve always thought Wuffli and Ospel were a team of the future,” says Hans Geiger.
"Wuffli, the highly intelligent, modest one who knows a lot about the models and statistics and Ospel, a person following the feelings around the stomach, who has evolved from the banking science completely up."
But Ospel is said to relied on Wuffli towards the end.
And Wuffli at the end probably made a decisive mistake: "He probably thought that his models were a reality," Geiger says.
"This is a tragic case."
Tragic, perhaps.
It is clear: Pride has preceded the fall.
UBS employees clearly recall executive meetings, at which Wuffli's chief executive repeated the same slogan over and over and over again: Knock Goldman Sachs off the Throne.
The U.S. bank was the most powerful investment bank in the world.
