Smart Crows Expand in Cities
In many places, field ravens are considered "problematic birds": however, these highly intelligent animals can no longer be expelled from the cities, experts say.
One likes them, others would like to get rid of them quite quickly: Crows belong to the image of the city for centuries, yet in some villages there are accumulate complaints of the inhabitants to the dirt and noise caused by animals.
At the same time, there is still no means in sight against "problematic birds".
Field ravens probably cannot be expeled from the towns, experts at the first symposium on crows in the East of Emir, East Frost, said.
There experts from all over the Federal Territory on possible starting points were consulted.
They are looking for alternatives in the cities
Conservation defenders also derive the problems of cities from the exodus of specially protected ravens of fields: The use of chemistry in agriculture probably increasingly destroys traditional areas for life.
Purchasing, growing buildings and felling of typical trees to nesting birds make it difficult to live.
Crows are protected.
Large colonies with more than 600 nesting pairs, among other things, are in the Westphalian Soesto, the north-sweather, among others in Jever, Diepholz and Achim near Bremen and Leer.
There the 2005 controversial project of mass killing of cranes in a rural county provoked negative submarines.
Critics have labelled the capture of the animals in traps for birds and the killing of sticks as particularly brutal.
"Normal expulsion, scaffolding, doesn't bring anything up," urban planner and landscape designer Werner Klöver of Leer found so much.
Remembrancing nests by squirting water or felling trees often has undesirable consequences, environmental expert Manfred Kaiser of Lahr in Baden-Württemberg also said.
"Animals recede, great colonies are splitting and dividing into other places."
Equation is conceivable only as an exception for hospitals, nursing homes or schools.
"We've got to live with them, we don't get them away anymore."
Without the surrounding area, the problem of the cities cannot be dealt with, the opinion of Mayor Wolfgang Kellner from Leer.
Conceivable is a combination of expulsion of animals from sensitive residential areas and attracting them to settle in other places.
"Information boards instead of chainsaws" was promoted, on the other hand, the psychologist and ethnologist Uta Maria Jürgens due to a completely different treatment of animals: "In the end, we can best withstood self-confidence."
Mrs. Jürgens co-founded an apprenticeship in Schleswig-Holstein Asscherberg, on which information boards provide an indicative of the life of a highly intelligent field raven.
In the meantime, the project has already been accepted by tourists.
Mrs. Jürgens also held her own opinion on the criticism of the noise caused by birds: "It's about every volume of communication, they have a lot to say to each other."
The intelligentness and skilfulness of animals are legendary: Field ravens don’t only take advantage of tools in order to get a treat, but even make their instrument as an aid.
Researchers from the University of Cambridge recently tested behavior on four ravens of field, how far it reaches the intelligence of the birds.
In the same experiment, the birds quickly learned that the stones had to cause a collapse of the structure in order to reach the larva of the catwalk.
Without a lot of training, they could know what size and shape the stone must have in order to collapse the platform.
In the second attempt, cunning crows then had to solve a predatory problem.
In a vertically-developed cylinder, the researchers placed a tiny jar with a moth larva.
With a beak, the birds couldn’t reach their prey.
However, the researchers placed pieces of wire for them.
From them she shaped all the four animals a hook, with the help of which she hunted the jar behind the handle from the pipe as a cloth.
Three of the four field ravens managed to do this even with a jam.
