Massacre on a personal level
“Green Beauty and The Sorrow: An Intimate History of the First World War” begins with such a long list of performers when you get dizzy.
One piece of advice on how to behave at that moment: Carefully pluck this page and turn it into a bookmark.
You will get acquainted very well with all the persons in Mr. Englund’s nobility and this list will serve as a navigation – as a kind of beacon – in moments when, like men and women in the story, you will feel confusing and lost in different situations.
Mr. Englund is a Swedish historian and journalist.
He is also a new deputies at the Swedish Academies awarding the Nobel Prize in Literature.
The book we're talking about here is very unusual - the author himself describes it, quite aptly, as an "anti-historical work".
It contains several well-familiar names, important treaties or glorious battles, on the contrary it almost lacks ambassadors, energetic journalists as well as debates about tactics and equipment.
It's still not a book about what's happened, the author's explains, like "a book about what it's been like". It deals mainly with "feels, impressions, experiences and moods".
"beauty and sadness" combines wartime experiences of 20 more or less dozen men and women, on both sides a barricad, from students and botanists to mountaineers, doctors, paramedics and clerks.
Some of these people will become heroes.
Others become captives of war, they will lose their limbs, go crazy or die.
Their lives flicker like the flames of candles lit up in a church - the new ones are added to the intricacies, while others extinguish.
Mr. Englund's book differs from classic historical novels.
Advises the notion that the First World War was only about terrifying trench warfare on the Western Front.
"beauty and sadness" skillfully explores other places where war dramas took place: the Alps, the Balkans, the Eastern Front, Mesopotamia, East Africa.
Soldiers in this book solve the problems that bee nests fall on them, they have to spend Christmas in the shadow of the pyramids, or struggle with the presence of flies tse-tse.
Almost from the very beginning, it is a very touching read.
The war engulfs people's lives as some kind of natural catastrophe, like Hurricane Katrina smeaning with gunpox.
When shooting from guns begins to sound in the distance and you are a woman who is at home with her children, you run away or stay away?
- Who's coming?
Almost no one understands what's going on, not even why the war was unleashed.
"Vacuum resulting from the lack of facts," Mr. Englund noted, "has been filled with controversies, conjectures, hopefuls, fears, prejudgments, conspiratorial theories, dreams, nightmares and rumors."
"beauty and sorrow" follows the stories of individuals, such as Florence Farmborough, an English nurse in the Russian army, and Richard Stumpf, a young sailor serving in the German fleet.
Their stories are mostly taken from biographies, letters and other materials already published.
Describs of their lives may sound terrifying and eye-cautiously, but by incorporating tiny moments and details, they are absolutely vivid and believable.
Many of them deal with hardship and collusion.
In the story of a German student, the author says: "Replacements, substitutes everywhere."
"Coonade substitute, imitation of aluminum, imitation of rubber, paper dressings, wooden knobs."
It goes even further and describes food substitutes in Germany.
Mentates "meose made from pressed rice cooked in mutton fat (and supplemented with a dulcist bone made of wood), tobacco from dried roots and dried potato peels, shoes with wooden soles".
Mentions there were "837 registered meat substitutes when making sausage and 511 registered coffee substitutes".
Some stories are about honor and bravery.
One American finds inner impulses for ferocity and brutality, whereupon he proclaims about the war: "Finally, you will understand that this is human nature. Not sitting in comfortable armchairs with cigarettes and whiskey, an evening newspaper or a good book, and to pretend that this masculine of yours represents a civilization, and that there is no barbarian hiding behind the starchy self inside."
On the other hand, the British soldier realizes that he will most likely die soon and no one will even notice, let alone grieving.
“When one finally comes to reconsider the fact that you’re sacrificing, you’d at least expect it to happen in front of a grateful audience,” he writes.
Instead, however, he feels like a "convicted man he secretly hangs".
Further remembrance relates to the passing of ancient Europe and the emergence of new horrors.
"Conflict became more and more an economic competition and a competitive fight," writes Mr Englund, "a war between the factories."
Capturing what he calls "a new kind of beelie of the coming century: an eloquent and ideologically sworn mass murderer in a bespoke-tried dress that performs its bloodbath without getting up from the table."
People are behaving completely unexpectedly, wickedness alternates with heroism.
Mr. Englund also describes the soldiers who deliberately tried to catch some venereal disease from prostitutes in order to avoid service at the queue.
"The most grotesque example of this behavior was the gonococo ploy, which the soldiers were buying and rubbing the genitals in the hope that he would end up in the hospital," he writes.
"The most desperial ones intruded into their eyes, which often blinded them for the rest of their lives."
In Peter Grave's Swedish translation, Mr. Englund's prose is brisk, but ostentatious, which suits his human theme perfectly.
In dozens of tiny outcomes, it captures the way the war "unleased completely unmancipable forces: extreme nationalism, social revolution, religious hatred".
People are beginning to wonder why their leaders force them to fight.
The best books about the First World War mostly, rather than complex narration, worked with hints, such as "World's First War and Modern Remembrance" by Paul Fussell or novels such as "In the Western Front Quiet" by Erich Maria Remarque.
Mr. Englund prefers an unconventional concept.
His book has the most devaluable ending that is unparalleled in popular-educational literature.
But I won't tell you.
However, you will feel as if suddenly the author jumped out of the book, shrouded the page from the list of heroes from his hand and lit it.
After a previous mistake this time for a minute, a precisely planned raid
Hundreds of police officers took part, some in the helmets used in street unsituations.
The night from Monday-Tuesday was chosen because Zuccotti park should almost be vacant at that time.
No one knew about the action except a few high-profile officers. The others were first told that it was an exercise.
Police commissioner Raymond W. Kelly was at the forefront of the action, stressing how many problems the police department had to face during the operation.
Were not allowed to reconcile the cases in recent weeks when, for example, there was a use of pepper sprinkles against demonstrators, thereby violving the rules of the department and, moreover, a wave of sympathies spilled over to the side of the squatters.
That's why a police operation aimed at clearing Zuccotti park from protesters was started only after two weeks of careful planning and practicing.
The executives were inspired by operations taking place in other cities.
A massive exercise in the event of crisis-situation behavior took place in Randalls Island, which was preparations for an event in Zuccotti park.
Senior officials have intensified so-called unrest-suppression training - anti-treater measures involving the rapid transfer of a large number of police officers - with a focus on Lower Manhattan.
The last part of the training took place on Monday night, on the Manhattan side of the East River.
Orders to move to the park came literally "at the last minute," said someone to whom the event was originally explained only as "exercise".
"I'm acquainted a couple of police officers who have also been summoned to this action, and no one has definitely told them that they are going to Zuccotti Park," said a person who only spoke on the condition that he remained in anonymity.
"Only the highest-profile members of the department knew about going to Zuccotti Park."
One because of secession was a lesson that the city has already received once.
On October 14, city officials planned to clean up the park, but then they backed away from the plan, because as soon as the demonstrators heard about it, hundreds of them rushed into the park prior to the start of the operation.
Tuesday's action was attended by police officers from various units, including intervention groups from all over the area - dozens of police officers who are commonly used to strengthen neighborhoods with a higher level of crime.
Mr. Kelly explained that a large number of people are coming into the park during the day, so one o'clock at night was the perfect time to start the event.
"It was advisable to do this at a time when there will be as few people as possible in the park," he said.
Pike Slip and Franklin D. Roosevelt Drive were lined up on the border of Pike Slip and Franklin D. Roosevelt Drive, near the Manhattan Bridge, trucks of emergency units with arrogant lamps and amplions.
Lights and recorded news roaming the amplions scare away many protesters.
When members of the riot units entered the park in their blue windbreakers, many demonstrators packed their belongings and left.
The police claim that until 1:45 a.m. they didn't touch a single tent until 1:45 a.m. to give the protesters the time to pack their belongings.
Other teams of police officers were spotted gathering on the outskirts of the park so they could break into it if any arrests had to be carried out.
The reporters were forced to leave the park.
Chief police spokeswoman, Paul J. Browne, argued that this was the case for their safety.
However, many reporters are of the opinion that the police wanted to prevent them from seeing its troops in action, and moreover, some police officers behaved coarsely towards them.
Mr. Browne argued that television cars on Church Street leading along the park's western boundary could easily be filmed.
The police were advocating in the west and intertwined with a dense tangle of the protesters' personal belongings, including luggage and plastic bags full of clothes. Behind them went teams from the sanitary ward, who immediately raked everything that was left in the park.
Some demonstrators who refused to move were dragged out of the park. Dedicating pictorial materials emerged on the Internet shortly thereafter.
The craziest-capped group of demonstrators retreated near the kitchen in their camp, which was located near the center of the park.
Some created a roadblock and the police started their methodical arrests.
About ten people at the center of the campsite tickled each other to each other.
And two people tied themselves to a tree, Mr. Kelly said.
Dependiency service members were summoned to cut locks.
Until 3:30 in the morning there were no arrests in the park, Mr. Kelly announced.
The whole operation was completed about 75 minutes later, the police said.
Mr. Browne said about 142 people had been detained.
Most of the arrest took place due to "organization of riotings and resisting arrest," he clarified.
The highest-ranking police officer at the scene of the event was Joseph J. Esposito, the commander of the police department, the highest-ranking uniformed officer at the ward.
Phil T. Pulaski, the commander of the detectives at the ward, also personally took part in the operation.
Mr. Kelly was present, but "he didn't participate in managing the operation," Mr. Browne said.
A second group of police officers waited along the east side of Broadway, between Liberty Street and Cedar Street, in the event that it had to be summoned.
On Cortlandt Street, the block north of the park, and on Pine Street, a block south of the park, were erected roadblocks.
From these places, the police then unfurled a protective belt even further from Zuccotti.
Officers were spotted in one of them, many of whom were equipped with shields, pushing people even further across this border.
There were about 28 people on the northern border.
The wildest events of the night took place south of the park.
Around five o'clock in the morning he was spotted a protester south of Pine Street, jumping on the hood of a police car, and several others unleashing the air from the tire of a police van.
At one point, a piece of plywood flew out of the crowd.
At the end of the supper, one police officer was hospitalized.
Auxiliary Workers Trade Union Shall Punish City for Redemption
Trade unions representing hundreds of school auxiliaries who lost their jobs last month plan to sue the city on Wednesday. They alleviate that redundancies weren't necessary and, moreover, discriminated against disproportionate impact on schools attended by poor students.
Judiciary is probably the last option left to unions in the District Council 37 in their quest to overturn a lay-off decision.
Six hundred and seventy-twenty-two-thirty-two, parental coordinators, parental coordinators and other employees lost their jobs on October seven, who were among the worst-paying employees of the city. Their dismissal was described as recompense by the city for not allowing him access to a health care fund managed by platters last spring, through which the city wanted to seal a hole in the budget.
City officials categorically dismissed the allegations.
Speaking last month, Education Minister Dennis M. Walcott said the dismissal was part of a budget approved in June.
One of the members of the board immediately contravened the fact that there was no specific mention of the release of auxiliary workers.
Representatives of the District Council 37 have announced that they have already filled out the necessary documents and have warned the Ministry of Education that they will sued. They will convene a press conference on Wednesday where they will formally announce a lawsuit with the Supreme Court of State in Manhattan.
Prosecutors in the process will be Mr Walcott and the Department of Education, while the plaintiffs will be eight laid-off workers, all of whom are African-Americans or Hispanics, as well as most other assistants who have lost their jobs.
Elizabeth Thomas, a spokeswoman for the city's legal department, said the city hadn't yet seen the lawsuit, so she wouldn't comment on individual allegations.
According to a proposal for the New York Times, the trade unions focused on the amount of dismissed in the poor parts of the city, wanting to support their allegations of unfair conduct. They take the notion that poor schools have lost more assists because they have long been denied sufficient funds.
They point out that, while there weren’n’t at all being dismissed at all in schools in the Staten-Ocland district, in the Upper East Side, in Brooklyn, in District 23 in Brownsville they lost the jobs of 17 school assistants, and 46 assistants were dismissed in parts of District 8 and 9 in the Southern Bronx.
Reminds that five of the eight assistants lost their jobs at Public School 36 in Harlem, even though it is a school where 68 per cent of students live in poverty. The lawsuit says: "This isn't a neighborhood full of movable parents, who can procure funds to offset a budget deficit."
Public School's 36 budget has been down 3.26 per cent this year, and the school also lost its money when the city has changed the method of deducting bonus funds for every logged-in poor student.
Among other things, the trade unions argue that secondary school principals may have been confusing in a way to write a document authorising the dismissal of parental coordinators.
The word "excess" was used to describe the action, i.e. to abolish the position, even though this word is much more often used to describe the transfer of the teacher from the payroll of the school to the database of available workers whose wages are paid by the central authority.
"Sixty-six parental coordinators have been dismissed," the complaint's suggestion said, "and not just moved to a database as available workers."
The mission is coming to an end, the congress is ready to accept an agreement on the situation in Libya
At last, the Republicans and Democrats in Congress seem to agree on something related to the uprising in Libya.
However, before this happened, the revolution had to succeed, for a long time the ruling dictator Muammar Gaddafi had to be captured and slain, and the involvement of U.S. troops was terminated.
On Tuesday, the Commission for International Relations of the Senate will decide on statements supported by President John Kerry (Mass.) with Senators John McCain (Ariz.) and Joseph I Lieberman (Conn.), in which it should congratulate the Libyans on their successful insurgency and to thank U.S. troops for "wastefulness."
"With this statement, we want to applaude the coherence of the inhabitants of Libya, who began to rebuild their country," Kerry said.
"Both parties of the political spectrum would like to express support for their democratic endeavors."
These sentences may look innocent, but no debate related to Libya until now has not been easy.
Over the course of the summer, legislators from both sides steadily argued among themselves and the White House about the wording of a series of declarations for approval or, concurrence, the military role of the United States in the NATO mission in Libya.
In June, Kerry and McCain presented a resolution authorizing the limited use of U.S. Armed Forces in Libya.
The proposal was dismissed in the Chamber of Deputies due to the different opinions of both political parties, so it was never even voted on in the Senate.
However, on the same day that this proposal was rejected in the Chamber of Deputies, the chamber also rejected a measure to halt the financing of U.S. operations in Libya.
In doing so, the U.S. mission in Libya literally found itself in legislative purgatory - Congress criticized the way President Obama manages the mission, but it clearly did not irritate its members enough to stop supporting him in his actions.
The heaviness of Congress was eventually overtaken by other events.
Rebel conquered Tripoli in August and Gaddafi was killed on October 20.
The Nato mission officially ended on October 31.
Now the senate may be ready to act.
“Congratulations to the citizens of Libya for their immeasurable courage and admirable tenacity they have spent in an attempt to liberate their people” and “praises men and women of the armed forces of the United States and their allies, who engaged in an operation to protect the Libyan population, for extraordinary bravelgence and professionalism.”
The United States' national self-interception in a successful and irrevocable transition to democracy in Libya also confirms.
Will the new proposal gain the full support of the Commission for international relations, or will the legislators rescheduled into two halves, as was the case when approving the June proposal?
Senator Richard G. Lugar (Ind.), the Republican Party's main representative on the commission, opposed the latest version.
Lugar's spokeswoman said on Monday he hadn't yet seen the final version of the new proposal.
There will be a vote in the Chamber of Deputies this week on an amendment to the Constitution, which would require a balanced budget, which should be the highest legislative priority of almost all Republicans and Democrats in Congress.
This measure, promoted by Bob Goodlatt (Va.), will be the first amendment to the Constitution relating to a budget or other theme that will be voted on by the entire House or the Chamber of Deputies.
For the time being, other legislators have not been able to push their motions all the way to the poll.
Goodlatt's proposal is only one of the 68 amendments to the constitution, which were presented in the congress and touched a wide range of different themes.
Some of them were the same - the same proposal submitted to both the Chamber of Deputies and to the senate - and others are only minor variations of others.
Only in the House, you can find at least 15 versions of an amendment to a balanced budget and an additional handful that would like to "regulate federal spending."
While some of the proposals are identical, but their proponents - especially budding Republicans, who want to improve their reputation - may boast their "authorship".
Decree on budget regulation, which was approved in August, was required by both the Chamber of Deputies and the senate to vote on an amendment to a balanced budget.
In March, 58 senators voted in favor of a non-binding declaration in support of such an appendix.
In addition to the budget, several members also want to discuss restrictions on the number of periods in which members of the congress could serve, while José E. Serrano (N.Y.) wants to increase the number of periods allowed by the president.
Deputy Jesse L. Jackson Jr. (Ill.) offered a number of appendices that, among other things, guarantee the right of quality education and medical care.
Continuous proposal remains an amendment prohibiting same-sex marriage as well as an appendix prohibiting the burning of the national flag.
Some legislators also want to allow federal laws to annum if two-thirds of states stand up against them.
Although the members of Congress appear to be very active in designing various additions, compared to previous years, they do not deviately devially from the average.
In the 121st Congress they offered 77 amendments, while in the 100th Congress there were 66.
Their activity even fades compared to the early 90's of the 20th-century, when, according to the statistics of the senate library, members offered more than 150 amendments every two years.
But they weren't much more successful than current politicians.
The last appendix, which was added - the 27th amendment - was ratified in 1992, but in fact it was proposed by Congress in 1789.
