The Walls Have Eyes: How Researchers Execureate You on Facebook
Until Anders Behring Breivik became the new face of right-wing extremism in Europe, he was just another young man who presented his anti-immigrant views on the Internet.
Speaking Saturday, Braivik, who pleaded guilty to the July murdering rampage, after which there are 77 deaths in Norway, took part in his first public court hearing.
Although Breivik could have acted alone, he certainly wasn’t lone in cyberspace: he spent a lot of time at the computer while preparing an attack, chating with some of the millions of nationalists supporting right-wing groups on social media sites.
After this year's tragedy, scientists want to learn more about these people.
But how to find them?
Easily - you just have to go to Facebook.
"We're aware that getting them isn't really that hard at all," says Jamie Bartlett, lead author of a recently released report by the British research team on European digital populism.
The supply of personal information on Facebook is so comprehensive, Bartlett says it was a good idea for the researchers to simply take advantage of an ad on the site and, with absolute precision, determine the sought-after demographic group - something that marketing workers have been doing for years.
Bartlett's team across Europe found half a million fans of right-wing groups, whereupon they focused their attention and sent them advertisements. However, instead of inviting them to a newly formed group or offering them some dietary products, they urged them to fill out a questionnaire in which there were questions related to the level of their education, the relationship to violence and optimism given their own future.
Through Facebook Bartlett and his colleagues managed to get results from more than 10,000 fans of 14 different right-wing groups in 11 European countries - without having to leave the office at all.
"It's an entirely new way of conducting research," Bartlett notes.
Some of the results aren’t that surprising: on-line supporters of right-wing groups are mostly young men, immersed by an increasing rate of immigration.
But on the other hand, "threes who combine their online activism with offline activism are more sensible, more democratic and less violent than those who remain constantly hidden behind a computer's screen."
DEMOS team's work is just one example of how Facebook becomes a new tool in the hands of scientists.
Research teams, health scientists and political researchers use this site to study everything, from health problems to social trends that hide status updates, wall posts, and pressed “Like” buttons.
“Facebook reports more than 800 million active subscribers. On average, each of them will provide three different data on a daily basis, making this social network literally an “information supernova” that is behind the development of many new researches. The number of academic jobs in whose name the word “Focal Facebook has risen to almost 800% five years ago.
For some scientists, the magic of Facebook is that it gives them the opportunity to study people without bothering them somewhere on the street.
"Since it was true that when you wanted to carry out a possology among people from the British National Party, it was very difficult to identify them."
"You'd have to get to a party where you'd never let go," Bartlett says.
"That's what Facebook breaks down - you're just walking right next to them."
Others argue that the site can offer a way to identify a socio-health-health problem and escalate.
In a recent study by Professor Megan More of the University of Wisconsin, the research team found that university students who dismantled their drunken outputs on Facebook were much more at risk of the alcoholism problem than the students who were silent on the subject.
Morenová proposes that student peers monitor the site and come up with an offer to help the student, who publishes too many drunken statuses.
"The problem can't be solved if you can't diagnose it," Dimitri Christakis, co-author of Dr. Moren and director of the Center for Health, Behavior and Development of Children at the Children's Research Institute at the Seattle Children's Research institute.
"We've found a way to identify endangered children we wouldn't otherwise have a chance of discovering."
“Photo’s Facebook constantly probosses the data of its users.
The information team of the site drew up statistics on the context of relations statuses and Valentine's Day, voter turnout in 2010 and the coefficient of satisfaction of the nation (mainly with the use of the information to which they had access - proponents of Internet freedom have long argued that the site should make its information treasure more accessible to others).
But Facebook's users know they're being tracked, whether they like it or they don't - in exchanging the opportunity to play Scrabble with a friend who's thousands of miles away, you need to provide your personal information to the site.
But what if Facebook users become part of a research they don't even know?
Scientists have clearly defined rules on how to collect off-line data, but on-line data is accessible to everyone.
“I didn’t even think the majority of users thought that scientists could look into their profiles,” said Neil Selwyn, a sociologist at the London Knowledge Lab, a 2006 study he carried out using his students’ public Facebook walls.
"As far as they were concerned, they took it that the information is only between them and their friends."
Subsequently, it is also to consider the issue of the methodology.
There is no guarantee even in off-line research that the subject is completely honest.
It is not possible to verify in any way on Facebook how largely the information on the profile and the posts on the wall are true.
“Which you’re saying on Facebook and what you’re doing outside of Facebook, that’s two totally distinguished things,” Selwyn said.
That’s why Selwin, who has spent years researching on new technologies, thinks that the pencil and notebook continue to remain the best possible tool that can be exploited.
"When you go out into the real world and talk to real people, it's something that can't be replaced," he says.
"French research should be about society and societal activities - and a hell of a lot of social activities are still taking place off-line."
"Hoddy and inhuman": Anders Behring Breivik participates in his first public court rehearsal
Anders Behring Breivik, a man who pleaded guilty to orchestrating the two attacks during which 77 people were killed in Norway this summer, during his first public Judicial Judicial Shear in Oslo described himself as "resistance fighter".
