“I raise an objection to the Court of Justice, because the mandate was given to you by organizations supporting the ideology of hatred and because it promotes multiculturalism.”
"I confess my deliberations, but I don't accept any guilt for them."
Judge Torkjel Nesheim Breivik interrupted during this monologue because "he didn't want to give Breivik an opportunity to use this hearing as a stage from which to pronounce his views."
For the same reason, Breivik, who had received a prepared speech with him, refused to allow him to address the relatives of his victims at the end of the hearing.
Nesheim also indulged the police for Breivik in custody for the next 12 weeks, forbade him of any contact with the media for 4 thirties, and announced that the final authorities will have complete control over all his visits and all correspondence for eight weeks.
Hearing was attended by about 30 relatives of victims and surviving survivors.
Some have come in the hope that they would be able to globigate the Breivik's eyes, others in order to make sure he was being held tightly locked.
They all took part in order to move the case towards an imminent closure.
"It seemed to me that he looked chilly and inhumane," he told the NRK commentator one of the surviving attacks.
"It wasn't a pleasant thing, but for myself I can say that after I've seen and heard the suspect, I've mentally shifted again a little bit further."
Breivik, in a dark-shouile suit and a blue tie, remained calm and professional all the time. On arrival and going to the building without hassle-looked for journalists and survivors.
Contributing Breivik’s bizarre notion that he is part of a larger “respear” movement, the judge suggested that he was not insane, remarking that there was no evidence that he had an accomplice.
For some it may be a consolation that the June 22 attacks appear to be the work of a single ex-expectant and not a group of radicals.
However, this does not automatically reduce the grief and confusion, which are still in the minds and hearts of an entire nation.
"I'd wish it would fall out as a monster, but that's not the case," a relative of one of the victims said.
"If you did, everything would be much simpler."
7 Lessons from a Lost Interview with Steve Jobs in a Movie-July This Week
As soon as Steve Jobs died on October 5, the opinions of the people who compared him with Henry Ford began to appear - and elected him as his genius The Greatest Modern inventor.
On the recently rediscovered interview, which has been dusted off for a special film heading to cinemas this week, it will be the fact that his creative mind perfectly understood the business processes and working streams behind its products.
He was an astonishing designer, a dreamer and self-appropriating member of the hippie movement, but at the same time a pedantic organizer and a man who tunes every little detail - he believed that many entrepreneurs suffer "the idea that a really great idea is 90 percent of their work... [but] between an excellent idea and an excellent final product, there is still a long way to go a lot of work."
In 1995 Bob Cringely collaborated on the TV program Triumph of Computer Fools, which dealt with the dawn of personal computers. During filming there was a unique, more than an hour-long conversation.
At that time, it has been ten years since Jobs was forced to leave Apple, and had his hands full with his new computer company, NeXT, looking impatiently to see how the general public would accept the internet.
In the end, there was only a short part of Cringely's full conversation on the TV show and the original copies of the interview were said to have been lost while being transported.
Shortly after Job's death, VHS copies were found in the director's garage.
It is this material, slightly edited, based on the 68-minute film Steve Jobs: The Lost Interview, which is heading to selected theaters this Wednesday and Thursday.
For someone who is famous for his distaste for longer interviews, Jobs seems surprisingly enthusiastic that he can talk about his technological philosophy and trading strategies.
It also turns out to be a very thoughtful person.
It happens four times during an interview that there will be a longer pause between the question and the answer - 10 to 15 seconds of thinking, when you can see how Jobs perfectly thinks the answer so that it is the most appreciable and accurate.
There are also moments when Jobs’s perfectionism comes to the surface, when Cringely’s questions seems impatient and exalting him to reach more important issues and themes faster.
The conversation was filmed even before the era of Pixar, the iPod or the iPhone, and there are terrifyingly prophetically prophetic ideas posing when Jobs assesses what other companies are doing well and badly while offering its own vision of the future in the field of computer technology.
It's one thing it's to read the posthumous assessments of Jobs' career, but there's something raw and inspiration on Jobs here as an age-wrenching dreamer.
It has its own vision of what things might and should look like.
It didn’t take long, and after uttering his ideas, he also began to put them into practice.
A quick summary of seven scenes from the Lost Interview, which is definitely worth noticing:
How he learned to run a business:
"Driving business, I've always asked: Why are you doing this? and the answer was always the same: Well, that's just how it's just done."
“Nobody knows why he’s doing what he’s doing, he’s never going to think about business-to-do things.
That's what I found out.
Jobs goes on to describe his efforts to simplify accounts at Apple.
He was utterly confusing from the manner in which costs were recorded - they often started with the inscribished "standard costs", their amount could only be imagined. At that moment, he decided to go his own way - he developed an automatic tool that guaranteed that they could determine the commercial costs in a moment.
How he made a mischief for the pope:
Jobs recalls the "Blue Box", which he constructed with Steve Wozniak - a device that allowed people to effectively hack into the phone company and call anywhere in the world free of charge.
There's been a lot about the device in the past, but Job's enthusiasm, when he describes the Blue Box as an instrument of power is literally contagious.
It tells how he and Wozniak tested the device using a paid phone and during a call they allowed themselves to be switched from one AT&T network to another, while using as many satellites as possible.
"At least half-dozen times we intertwined this way around the world, so when we shouted something into the paid phone, it came a minute later from the second phone at the door," Jobs laughs.
"We've been young, and we've found out that we can build a billion-dollar-billion-dollar-grown-event infrastructure ourselves."
The two of us made a tiny thing that could control a much larger giant - it was an incredible experience, and in my opinion Apple would never have existed without it.
Jobs continues with a description of the prank she and Wozniak almost brought to an end. Through the Blue Box in the middle of the night, they called the Vatican and asked if they could talk to the pope, posing as much as possible under Henry Kissinger.
Gradually they were summoned and higher-ranking members of the Catholic Church to talk to an U.S. diplomat in the middle of the night, and it wasn't until they aroused the pope on the phone that the pair laughed and put the phone down.
How he fell in love with technology (and on the phone with Bill Hewlett):
When he was 12 and was looking for spare parts so he could build an oscillating machine, he found a Bill Hewlett number in the phone book and called him.
Not long after their 20-million-dollar interview, Jobs had already secured a summer brigade at Hewlett-Packard.
"Unbelievably it influenced me. It was the only company I met at the time, shaping my point of view at what such a company actually is and how to treat its employees."
Jobs later became one of HP's employees who went to Palo Alto, where he spotted "the first desktop computer that was ever made."
He was like a suitcase, he had a CRT monitor, and I immediately fell in love with it.
As a temper, I've progressed higher-and-a-half at HP, hanging around the computer, and I started typing programs for him.
How he invents innovations:
In a few places Jobs talks during the interview about how he invents new products on the fly.
In the early days of his career, when he set out to sell a pair of printed circuit boards, a customer asked him to put together the entire computer for him.
At his disposal he only had 30 paid days, during which he had to devise, assemble and deliver the finished equipment.
When he was later at Apple, he recalls the problems they faced while developing a computer mice:
"Remember how we were arguing quitfully... they were yelling at me that it would take five years [build a mice that would cost $300] when I got tired of it, I came out and found a designer."
Ninety days later we were able to produce a perfectly reliable mice for $15.
How great companies run out of breath:
A long time ago, before the resurrection of Apple, Jobs prudently predicted that many industry leaders would falters in their vision and lose their market share.
"Suppose you're working with IBM or Xerox, so you're doing better copiers or printers, so what's next?"
You don't have a monopoly on the market, so the company couldn't be more successful.
At that moment, sales and marketing are already ruled, so companies are run by people who are in charge of just this, while people in charge of their own products are outmaneuvered from the decision-making process.
Technological gypsy, who led the company to its unshakable position, is now replaced by people who do not have an idea of good or bad products - dexiosuality and technical knowledge are necessary.
That's exactly what's happened at Xerox...
Xerox could literally own the entire computer industry.
It could have been ten times the size, it could have been Microsoft in the 1990s.
Instead of the victory, however, they took a loss.
How it sees innovation as a form of art:
"Between an exquivation and an excellent final product, there is still a long journey and a lot of work to do... and as the great idea develops, it also changes and grows."
During that time you will learn a lot about him.
You have to accede to a whole series of compromises - certain things you just don't forgive electrons, glass, robots or factories.
You have to keep in mind steadily 5,000 things - different concepts - and put them together...
In the end, it depends on the taste - you try to think of the best things that humanity has ever created, and in a certain way you are trying to translate them into what you yourself are doing.
Picasso has said that good artists are copied, but the best artists are stealing.
I've never been shrugged for stealing the best ideas.
Macintosh was so amazing and successful because the people who have worked in it were excellent musicians, poetics, artists, zoologists, historians and coincidentally the best computer experts in the world at the same time.
If it weren't for computers and new technology, they'd do amazing things in completely different industries.
Scalia and Thomas supper with opponents of the health care law at the time their case was dealt with by the court
On the day the Supreme Court sat behind closed doors to discuss the politically controversial question whether he would accept an objection to President Obama's health care law, two of his judges, Antonin Scalia and Clarence Thomas, took part in a ceremonial sustenator-sponsored by a law firm that would take part in the proceedings before the court.
The incident took place last Thursday, when all nine judges came together in order to discuss the motions filed.
One of the cases under discussion was a lawsuit filed by 26 states questioning the radical overhautication of health care, which was approved last year-on-a-statu (a bill that mobilized Conservative Activists around the world.
The judges have agreed to oversaw the lawsuit. In March, however, a 5.5-hour meeting is expected, the result of which will be further stirred by the presidential election, which is scheduled for 2012 and will be in full swing at the time of the decision.
A lawyer to stand up before the Court of Justice and will try to applaunte the law will most likely be Paul Clement, who served as U.S. Attorney General under George W. Bush.
Clement's law firm, Bancroft PLLC, was one of nearly two-and-distance companies that helped sponsor the Federalist Society's annual sustenance, which for many years engaged in advocating conservative legal principles.
Another company that sponsored the suspense, Jones Day, represents one of the trade associations questioning the law-demanding the so-called National Federation of Independent Entrepreneurship.
Another spun was the pharmaceutical giant Pfizer Inc, for which huge financial resources are at stake.
Dinner took place in a Washington hotel a few hours after a judicial discussion on this case.
Mitch McConnell, a top representative of the Republicans in the Senate and a confirmed opponent of the Health Care Act, attended it.
And introducing the guests? Scalia and Thomas.
It's nothing new: This duo of judges have been attending the events of the Federalist Society Group for years.
And it's certainly nothing that's inconsistent with the rules of ethics.
In fact, these judges are acknowledging the obligation to comply with the principles of conduct subject to lower federal judges.
However, if this was not the case, their behavior would certainly fall under principle C4, which states that "the judge can participate in actions that are currently related and other organizations to obtain funds, but the judge may not be a speaker, an honorable guest, nor may he be listed on the agenda of the event."
Nevertheless, the relationship between Scalia and Thomas to the two law firms in the case, as well as a company with a huge financial concern in this case, was richly enough to alert activists calling for ethics in the government.
"This shumidifying violation of ethics and indifference to the code of conduct is inconsistent with several magistrate's assertion that the supreme court is behaving according to the same rules that apply to all other federal judges," Bob Edgar, president of Common Cause said in a statement.
"Jugerians drank and supper for a social benefit, together with legal representatives who are awaiting the hearing of their case on trial."
Their presence and assistance in fundraising through this action undermines the feet with all claims of impartiality, which is unacceptable.
Scalia and Thomas expressed little sympathy against their critics and told them that they also seamlessly combine their judicial responsibilities with the actions of groups such as the Federalist Society.
Thomas' wife Ginni is a high-profile Conservative activist.
Conservatives also argue that Judge Elena Kagan has an ethetic problem and not Scalia with Thomas.
Kagan worked as attorney-at-law under President Obama when the first objections to the law appeared in court.
Critics Kagan are pushing her to renounce taking part in the case, claiming that at the same time she struggled so much for the law to be unbiased now.
Kagan didn't indicate she was about to resign.
