ADB bank urges Asia to help sal save the Eurozone
Asian Development Bank has urged India and China to be prepared to help save the Eurozone against its serious debt crisis, otherwise there could be an overall downturn in the global economy, which would also have a negative impact on the growth of economies in Asia.
Rajat Nag, the CEO of ADB-headed Manila-based ADB, has said that the world's two fastest-growing economies must "make everything they can" in order to help a regenerative recovery of the currency bloc through the International Monetary Firm or direct bilateral agreements.
He cautioned that Bric [Brazil, Russia, India, China] is monitoring all Europe's difficulties "too uncourageously" and declared that Asian financial assistance combined with European leadership and resources will help ignite the long-term collapse of the global economy.
"We're all in this together."
"So it suits everyone who could help Europe out of the crisis," he told the Financial Times during an interview at the World Economic Forum in Mumbai.
"Asia may be protected to a certain extent, but it certainly isn't Immune."
"So if China and India can help, they should definitely do that."
For several months it has been severely criticized by a severe debt crisis.
In the last few days, Italy's borrowing costs have risen dangerously, and the prime ministers of Italy and Greece have withdrew.
Increasing a debt crisis in Europe has raised fears that it could relaunch the global economy into a recession, and calls have emerged that the newly thriving front-familiar economies would come together and help the eurozone find a solution.
Mr. Nag predicted that all Asian support would be routed through the IMF, but noted that mutual assistance - such as the purchase of bonds of the European Union's financial bailout fund, an institution for European financial stability - would provide greater bargaining capabilities for Asian partners of European countries.
Anand Sharma, an Indian trafficult minister, said "India will do whatever it can," to help the Eurozone, because its own economy is now suffering from a drop in exports and a drying-up inflow of foreign capital.
"No one wants the Eurozone to remain unstable and restless," he said.
"We are faced with a difficult task and we have to maintain a high rate of growth."
"It's not an alternative, it's a bare fact, because otherwise we could find a job for tens of millions of our young men and women."
Others argue that Emerging economies, such as Indian ones, have nothing to help European economies when they are being compelled to face their own serious economic problems.
Ashutosh Varshney, a professor at Brown University in the U.S., said it would be politically very difficult to explain aid for Europe's 1.2-billion-popular population of India, when 800 million of them live from $2 or less per day.
"Once they could find that the Greeks retire in their 50s and are enjoying the holidays on the beaches, which it would probably be difficult for them to explain," he said.
Lee Howell, director-in-a-Chief of the World Economic Forum, has similarly questioned why Indian stockpiles should be used to maintain well-paying jobs for many Greek citizens in poorly managed and loss-going sectors such as rail transport.
Mr. Nag stressed that the Eurozone crisis is threatening a "dangerous domino effect".
The bank's prediction that €7.5% economic growth will occur in Asia between 2011/2012 is now facing a "disaggeration" because of the European threat.
He stated that the vulnerable emerging markets need to create "investigable spending plans" in order to be protected from a downturn and a significant outflow of capital from their economies.
Warning from the Pentagon about possible cuts in the field of defense
Defense Minister Leon Panetta put a hard-waller in Congress on Monday when he warned that looming automatic budget cuts could jeopardize national security and provoke a financial chain reaction starting at the Pentagon and continuing through the battlefield in Afghanistan to civilian assembly lines.
