Incredible climate has hampered the economy
Unemployment and unreliability rose in Sinaloa during the first-in-a-year reign of Mario Lópeze Valdeza
The year 2011 will go down in the economic history of Sinalo. This is the first year of Mario López Valdez's government, which itself is called a government of change. This government brought with it great anticipation in terms of economic progress, but due to unfavorable climatic conditions at the beginning of the year, there was no growth, and there was even a decrease in the main indicators of economic development.
The low February temperatures caused millions of losses in the field of agriculture, limiting the possibilities of the growth of the state economy, they are responsible for reducing economic activity by 3.6% in the first semester of this year. In the area of agriculture there was a historical decrease of 31.16%, which disrupted the dynamics of other economic sectors.
According to a report on damages issued by the Mexican Council for sustainable rural development, the damage was recorded on 582 835 hectares of cultivated land, of which 78.03% have been completely destroyed and partial damage was found for the remaining 21.07%.
The decrease in economic activity until November caused a constant increase in unemployment in the state. According to the National Institute for Statistics and Geography, an unemployment rate in the state experienced an increased increase during the first three quarters. From January to March, 55 053 residents of the state of Sinaloa were unemployed, representing an unemployment rate of 4.53% an economically active population. In the second quarter, unemployment increased to 5.22%, and between July and August the average unemployment continued to rise.
Fewer liquidity
Unemployment during the year in Sinaloa is caused by several factors. According to Gerard López Cervantes, Director of the Faculty of Economics and Social Sciences of the UAS, it is possible to explain the increase in unemployment indirectly, based on the steps that have been carried out at public policy level.
"We are witnessing an increasing unemployment that is related to various factors. These include, among other things, the loss of liquidity in the market due to resource restrictions as a result of legislation approved by Congress in connection with the fight against money laundering. It is not an easy thing for people who illegally seize dollars to earn them, invest them or use them on something," he stressed.
Suspension of migration
López Cervantes has also stated that the demographic factor is also contributing to the increase in unemployment.
"The number of people retiring for work abroad has declined, on the one hand due to a difficult entry and also because even if these people get abroad, it is difficult for them to find a job. Therefore, people stay and demand work from our state, and so unemployment is rising," he said.
Unlucognidence
An analysis of the labour market in Sinaloa also deteriorates due to the growing unreliability that prevails in working conditions. According to the National Institute of Statistics and Democracy, 60.38% of employed residents in the state are working without distributing benefits to the Social Security Administration.
Unemployment and unreliability include other features of the labour market, such as low salaries. In this administrative territory, salaries from all over the country, according to the registrans of the Mexican Institute of Social Security and the Secrecy of Labor and Social Praiguration.
The average monthly income of workers in the state of Sinaloa until September was 5 352 peseta, well below the national average, which averages 7 375 pesset.
Summunication of foreign direct investment
Along with a decline in economic activity and an increase in unemployment in 2011, there was also the most pronounced decrease in foreign direct investment over the past 10 years. According to the Ministry of Finance between January-June, foreign direct investment reached $630 $6,697, a decrease of 87.74%, compared to $ 5 143 312 for the same period in the previous year.
On the other hand, the Ministry for the Economic Development of the Government of the state reports an investment of 17 280 million pieces per year. These investments were directed primarily to enterprises in the field of services.
Debt
In connection with the scenario of a downturn in the economy, the government of that state government in July envisaged the state congress to approve a loan of 3 billion peso. It would be earmarked for investments in infrastructure projects in 18 urban areas in order to revitalize the economy. The local congress approved a loan amounting to 2,600 million peso. By November, there would be no profits from debt, in any sector of the state economy.
Responsence
According to the representatives of the business sector, the first year of Mario López Waldés's government's first-year government rule pointed out the lack of government management skills.
Miguel Loredo López, Chairman of the National Chamber of Transformational Industries, declared that in 2011 there was a lack of reconciliation of the will of individual sectors, including the government of the state itself.
"Development of the economy in Sinaloa during this year was really sluggish. We have to work intensively to regain the ground we lost this year. In 2012, we have to double our efforts, the harmonisement of the efforts of all of us and, in particular, the proper and synchronized functioning of this new government was lacking. Let's hope that next year will be much better," he said.
Scalability of the economy
Loredo López said events like frost at the beginning of the year halted the state-owned economy and curtailing its development and growth.
"Weak economic movement was caused by a series of events, among other frosts. One of the main consequences was a slowdown of the economy, which did not recover quickly enough to restore dynamism, which would make it possible to achieve an acceptable level of movement of processes, products and services. That is why at the end of the year we found ourselves well below the expected forecasts," he said.
Redefinition of mission
According to Teodoro Salazar Uriarte, the president of the People's Employant Confederation of the Mexican Republic in Sinaloa, the state needs to be pressured for more dynamic productive activities.
"Generally speaking, we are obsessing ourselves. We believe it is necessary to prepare a long-term plan, in order to redefine the objectives and economic direction of the state, because it is impertinent to mobilize all the potential of the material and human resources that Sinaloa has," he said.
A job that's in front of us
Chairman Coparmex stressed the importance of establishing a vision of a future that would better guide the government's efforts.
"In general, I would say that the government was trying to achieve good results in various areas, there have been some progress in economic development, but it is important to create a long-term plan," he said.
He argued that the current administration has shown progress when it comes to conducting public policy.
"There are elements that make it possible to assert that the government is trying to be affiliate. I can say that the work done is seen here and I believe that the government is aware that there is also a lot of work ahead of us," he said.
