They owe contributions to the projects
Social projects, which are part of programs such as Hábitat, Rescuing public spaces and 3x1 for migrants, are facing the danger that they will not be brought to an end or that they will be delayed due to the lack of economic resources, Miguel Ángel López Miranda said.
The director of urban social development has stated that if the construction work has gone forward so far, there is now a risk that the government of the state will not be able to provide adequate deliveries, which require funds in the amount of more than 11 million peso for three programs.
He stated that the total investment in these three programs is almost 50 million pesos. The state accounts for 25%. By this time the funds for work were supplied only by the federation and the city, so it is possible that there will be a delay in the implementation of the projects.
"They are supposed to secure almost $12 million, and even though a year is coming to an end, we didn't get anything at all. Even though we were supposed to get a part already in July", he said. "In order not to waste our time, we have started the projects and are continuing very successfully, but we lack money".
He stated that last year under the administration of Jesús Aguilar Padilla, the state did not meet the appropriate deliveries and therefore at the beginning of the new period, the city had to apply for a loan in order to pay more than 7 million, which was not given to them by the state administration.
"The same thing happened to us already in the previous six-year period, because the state did not have the economic capacity to be able to participate and the programs went on. In order to get to the mentioned programs this year, we had to raise funds that would cover what the state did not finish," he explained.
Conceding that there is a promise by state officials regarding the transmission of these resources, he said a request to Armand Villarreal had been sent to the Secretary of the administration and the finances in order to refute the proceedings and the corresponding percentages arrive as soon as possible.
"They should've given us $12 million, and although the year is coming to an end, we haven't even gotten a peso yet."
The Court approves the "end of analogous transmission" in Mexico in 2015
Replacing analogous digital signals is one of the most important changes that the world has experienced in recent years.
The Supreme State Judiciary Court (SCJN) has dismissed a constitutional lawsuit submitted by the Chamber of Deputies against the President Decree, which wants to speed up the so-called "excaution of analogous signals" and implement it in 2015 instead of 2021. It stated that the necessary eight votes have not been found, which would invalidate it.
A minority of four presiding ministers, including the presiding judge, Juan Silva Mezo, believed that President Felipe Calderón did not exceed his powers when he handcuffed a decree listing individual steps that would lead to the transition to terrestrial digital television.
An action filed by the senate has been dismissed with this result, and this is because a qualifying majority of eight votes needed has not been obtained to allow the court to comment on the constitutionality or unconstitutionality of the decree, which thus remains in force.
The draft judgment drawn up by Minister Olga Sánchez Cordero presents the opportunity to declare the decree unavailable, because the regulation of all issues related to radio and television is a thing that, according to the law, is conserved by the Federal Commission for Telecommunications (Cofetel). This violates the principles of hierarchical subordinariness and an exception to the law.
Only Zaldivar, Jorge Mario Pardo, Margarita Luna and Sánchez Cordero themselves voted for the project.
With this arguing, the Chamber of Deputies supported a constitutional controversy in an quest to fight against the Decree. Minister Sánchez Cordero accepted the demands and suspended the application of the decree and all the actions described in it leading to the transition towards digitization.
The meeting of the ministers also decided to reject a lawsuit filed by the Chamber of Deputies, without entering a discussion on a proposal also in charge of Minister Sánchez Cordero.
Speaking, the prime minister stated that he did not agree with the arguments of the proposal in the sense that a federal executive could not structure through general norms of measures to prepare public administration.
“I don’t think that even if it did, it would affect the autonomy of the regulatory body,” and he recalled that public policy planning in strategic areas, such as the telecommunications sector, belongs to a federal executive who has a constitutional mandate to determine the direction of the state.
Reimbursement of analogous digital signals is one of the biggest changes that the world has experienced in recent years. It is related to the better use of radio-electrical spectra, improved signal quality, and multiple channels.
He argues that detachment of change would mean detach the benefit of digitization, which requires a decision on a matter of public interest. Digitization does not only have a technical dimension, but "applies also about economic, social, political and national security issues".
As he pointed out, these questions "require the operation of the entire established system, in accordance with the principle of the economic direction of the state. It includes a variety of competencies that the Constitution entrusts in the hands of the rule of the state".
Recalling that a number of countries around the around the world have already completed the transition to a digital system and did so through their governments, even though they had at their disposal highly specialised technical bodies.
He argued that it is not possible for the government by extraditing a dissenting Decree, which to some extent interferes with the powers of the congress, to introduce compliance with law-proprietary laws prepared by legislative power.
In Michacán they analyze the possibility of censing "voice after voice"
According to PREPE published, the difference between the first and second place in the governor's elections is less than 27 per cent. So far, 879 from 6074 constituencies have not been counted.
The possibility of censing "vote after the vote" appeared in this year's elections in Michoacán, where, in accordance with data published by the Preliminary Electoral Execution (PREP), the difference is between the first and second places in the governor's elections of less than 27 percent, and it has not yet counted the votes of 879 out of a total 6074 constituencies.
In this context, the chairwoman of the General Council of the Electoral Instance in Mikhoacán (IEM), María de los Ángeles Llanderal Zaragoza, recalled that a few days, on November 9, an electoral body approved a series of measures to harmonize local standards with federal electoral reform.
As can be recalled, Michoacán officially did not officially confirm his political constitution or his State Electoral Code or the Electoral Justice Act. The State Congress approved the reforms shortly before the legal deadline for their entry into force during this electoral period should have escalated and Governor Leonel Godoy Rangel omitted their publication within a specified period in the Act of the Official State Bulletin, stating that he had any objections.
On November 9, the Supreme State Court (SCJN) resolved a constitutional litigation initiated by local legislative power, declaring the voice of the head of state on constitutional reforms "invalid and illegal", because it does not have the right to "allow or veto them".
When asked about the possibility of counting "voice by voice" María de los Ángeles Llanderal replied that by approving the relevant measures by the IEM General Council it would be possible for a partial and total count to be carried out by a local electoral body.
In this way, the partial counting of votes, one or more electoral deputies, may be carried out under the following conditions: electoral results do not disagree; there is no record of the counting of votes and a calculation in the appropriate box, or if there have been errors and an obvious violation of the electoral results.
In Addition, the total recalculation of votes in all the ballot boxes may be carried out if there is an indication indicating that the difference between the potential victor of the election and the candidate in second place is smaller or equal to one percentage point; always when, at the beginning of the session, an exploratory request of a party representative and/or the Coalition that proposed the candidate in second place appears.
The total recalculation of votes can also be carried out when, after a proper addition, it is found that the difference between the likely winner and a candidate in the second place is equal or less than one percentage point, and also whenever there is an explicit request for a political party whose candidate finished in second place. In this case, electoral parcels will be disqualified, for which a partial census has been carried out.
It should be mentioned that in such a situation there is an election in Morelia, where, according to PREP, the difference between the first and the second place is less than 0.16%, although 1,29 of the total 923 electorcant districts have not yet counted.
His last will
Its ashe ashes will be scattered in the Zapaliname Mountains, the family confirmed.
Felip Bacio Cortés was transported to Saltillo, his hometown on Sunday night. A ceremonial ceremony and an ammuda mass will take place.
According to the family, the lieutenant colonel's last wish that its ashe ashes be dissipated in the Zapaliname Mountains.
After, along with other persons who were killed in the accident, Bacio Cortése's family returned to Coahuila's capital aboard a Mexican Air Force's jet. The family and closest friends took part in a ceremony at Campo Marte.
At noon the lieutenant-countal arbuilder arrived in the chapel located on Nazario Ortiz Garza, where family and friends also came.
At 5:30 a.m. there was a farewell at the Technology Institute in Saltillo, which was orchestrated by his former high-educated school classmates and members of the U.S. football team that Felipe Bacio was also a member at the time of his graduation.
"He wanted to go back to Saltilla, and his wish was that his ashe ashes were dissipated in the Zapaliname Mountains. That's what he always said to his family and friends," Sandra Bacio Cortés, the pilot's sauer.
According to her, the lieutenantman fulfilled all his wishes, he was at the height of his life, "he was a fabulous man, an extraordinary father, a son, a loving brother living for his family".
Felipe Bacio Cortés has retired from Saltillo and joined Colegio del Aire in Zapopán, Jalisco.
Some didn’t believe it, because they said he was even driving a truck, but he always went after his dream of becoming a pilot.
His nephew Jorge Alberto Dávila Bacio remembers him as a very good person, "he barely came to Saltillo, the whole family wanted to see him as it was when he brought Martha Sahogun to Saltillo".
The young man even recalls that when he was seven years old, he went to Zapobran to graduating his unhapplied, so he is convinced that he died in what he liked best in his life: driving a plane.
They gave him the last goodbye
With Felip Bacio Cortés, Lieutenant-Claucher of the Mexican Aerial Forces, we said good farewell to all the tribute that Governor Jorge Torres López, the citizens and the government of the state, the Mexican army through the Sixth Military Zone, the Mexican Air Force itself, the leaders and students of the Technological Secondary School in Saltillo, where he studied in 1985 and where he exceld in both his studies.
