The new Italian government will be appointed on Wednesday
Deputy Prime Minister Mario Monti will meet with the Italian president tomorrow to present him with a new government that will face a crisis that keeps this country over the abyss of economic catastrophe.
Deputy Prime Minister Mario Monti will meet with the Italian president tomorrow to present him with a new government that will face a crisis that keeps this country over the abyss of an economic catastrophe that has put into danger the entire eurozone.
The presidential palace has issued a report announcing that Monti, appointed on Sunday, will meet with President Giorgio Napolitano on Wednesday to reconciprove that he could form a government.
It is expected to introduce a government composed mostly of technocrats, although it is not yet clear when the new government takes power.
Monti has stated that he would present to the president the results of his talks, which he conducted with political parties in an attempt to form a government.
“I desire to confirm that I have an absolute confidence in our country’s ability to overcome this difficult phase,” he said.
Monti did not explicitly say that he would be able to form a government, but the tone of his comments implied that hurdles were overcome.
"The framework is now well-marked," he added.
Monti has finalized the process of forming a government in less than three-day, i.e. in a much shorter period of time than usual. In the meantime, Italy is trying to combat the political and financial crisis, due to which the amount of debt has climbed to an unsustainable level.
A new administration led by former European commissioner Montim must authorise a tough austerity-at-treason-determined bailout package demanded by European leaders. This is the only way to rebuild Italy's degraded trustworthiness.
There is a lot of exerted pressure on Monti to quench. Due to the instability of the markets, interest rates on Italian BTP bonds with a ten-year maturity have exceeded a 7 percent threshold, the level when Greece and Ireland had to apply for bailout.
Emma Marcegaglia, chairwoman of Italy's Confindustry Employance organization, told reporters after a meeting with Monti: "We have said that we will very much support this government."
We believe that this government is a last resort for Italy to get out of this burning situation.
Monti's hopes of success have multiplied significantly thanks to Silvio Berlusconi's PDL's PDL, who was compulsored by the Crisis to resign.
“We believe Professor Monti’s efforts are predestined for good results,” Angelina Alfano told reporters.
Promoting PDL, Italy's largest party, is significant, because up to this moment, a number of its members have been opposing a technocratic-senior-based government compiled by Monti.
Promoting the Parliament
Monti's new government must have strong support for parliament in order to promote unpopular austerity reforms.
Any obstruction or lag in a given effort could trigger a new and devastating attack on financial markets.
Italy's foreign bank association has added pressure and warns that Monti's failure could be a catastrophe.
Monti began talks with political parties, trade unions and business groups, youth organisations and women on Monday.
The round of negotiations will end on Tuesday night.
Monti has been appointed President Napolitano on Sunday, which allowed a very rapid procedure in response to the crisis.
After a brief downturn at the end of last week, when Berlusconi would retire, Italy's interest rates have returned to the critical threshold, responding to the uncertainty of the new prime minister's success.
Responsoring Italy, whose public debt is €1.8 trillion, would be beyond the options for the eurozone's current financial reserves.
Monti has stated that his government should stay in power until the next election, which is scheduled for 2013, despite general forecasts, which claim that politicians will only give him time to carry out the reforms and then declare early an election.
Monti has stated that he would like to integrate politicians into his cabinet, but the big parties insist that this government only needs to be compulsored by experts in order to lead Italy out of the difficulties that the country got into because of financial pressure.
Political sources have argued that reciprocal no-confidence and dissponsibilities complicate an incumbent attempt to integrate political figures into the government.
Spaniards participate in international matches more often than Costa Ricans
Of the 22 Spanish footballers, ten will surpass the limit of 50 international matches.
On the Costa Rica's side, only one of the 22-authorized players came to the number of 50 matches.
Huge continent on the Costa Rica side if we compare the 22 players of the two countries, who met today at a friendly match at 15:05 at the National Stadium in San José.
