Such was the work of the inhabitant of Cali, who identified Alfons Canoe's corpse
An expert on Cali fingerprints went into history as a man who affirmed "Alfonsa Cana."
The work of these clerks is crucial.
On November 4 last year, an CTI fingerprint expert in Cali went down in history as a man who affirmed the identities of Supreme Leader Farc: "Alfonse Canoe", by comparing fingerprints.
Of the seven Fingerprint experts working on the CTI department this week were Eliazar González, a man from Puerta Tejad, who has been working in the field of judicial research for 21 years.
At the beginning of his career, González worked at the town hall in Chocó, where he often spent more than five days laughing through the rainforest in order to get to a place where there were corpses that had to be identified.
He retired himself to the capital city of Valle in 2000 and, after completing the relevant courses, he began to specialize in fingerprints. This science is engaged in the study of friction of the skin, i.e. the palms of the hands and feet.
In the week when operations were carried out against Alfonso Can, Eliazar was ordered to take clothes with him for at least three days.
Something great was expected.
He set off for Cauca in a helicopter from Palmir on Friday night.
He knew that he would have to identify two corpses and that he suspects that one of them was the man who ran the oldest guerrilla in the world. That's why he was asked to take a card with Guillermo León Sáenz (his real name).
Nevertheless, when he arrived with the investigating team to the place where the corpse was located, he did not recognize it because of physical appearance. He looked completely different than in the media.
He didn't have a beard, he was dressed as a villager in a given area, without a spectacle, much slimmer.
But fingerprints don't lie.
Along with galton magnifying glass, ink and a transplantator and light, Eliazar began to compare dactyl carcasses with card-written.
The eye didn't let him down: "Alfonso Cano".
On the same night the man originally from Puerto Tejada was commissioned to testify to the Secretary of Defense, Juan Carlos Pinzón, confirm whether it was indeed the Chief Commander of Farc.
Looking for traces
There is a recognition to Eliazar González from Syjin de Cali at the press conference of operational services against "Cana" very important.
Plenty people don't know our work.
Dactyoscopy is an exact science, without the possibility of error, which can play a key role in decision-making crimes.
In addition to the identification of corpses, these people are assisted in the research of crimes such as the theft or homicide and confirm the identities of trapped persons.
When they are caught, a lot of delinquents present fake documents or don't have papers at all.
We take their fingerprints and look for them in the database.
Investigator retorted that in the event of an injury, he had to remove a piece of the wall of the house in the house, because in this place the burgeon was relied upon to pull out a vault full of jewellery and money.
The walls are difficult because they are not intended to collect fingerprints, but in this case we were able to get an entire handprint and identify the perpetrator who was involved in several thefts," he recalled.
Fingerprints can also confirm or refute suspicions in the event of murder.
They recently found a dead man with bottles of liquor.
On the basis of the investigation it was found that one of the family members could be responsible for the murder, and an imprint on the bottle actually confirmed it.
Although it can not be one hundred percent confirmed that he really was a murderer, it is obvious that he has appeared on the crime scene.
CTI's fingerprint specialist explained that objects "haping using chemical or physical reactive substances."
When it appears, a transplanter or tape is used for their collection.
After that, immissions are shipped to Asif, a database of delinquencies, or Registry.
"The problem is that there are still many Colombians, whose data is not recorded anywhere at the moment," the CTI expert said.
Explauding that it wasn't all as it turns out on the CSI television program.
Just as everything that shimmers isn't gold, it doesn't affect everything you touch.
However, if it can be used, an appropriate comparison must be carried out.
"Indignant" protests in New York
Hundreds of demonstrators were crowded in Zuccotti, where they wanted to camp.
A large of them awaited order to evacuate the space and moved to a nearby park.
Members of the Occupy Wall Street movement provoked New York officials on Tuesday as they tried to re-execute in Manhattan, from where they were escorted out in the morning. They then attempted to deprive another New York park.
Hundreds of demonstrators crowded in Zuccotti Square, where they wanted to reach thanks to an injunction that authorizes "indignant". The second part of the movement reacted to evacuation by seizing the park in the north of the city, where there was further detention.
"If the court does not allow us to go back to the square, we will settle in a different place and pursue a lawful battle in order to return to Zuccotti, either through gatherings and peaceful demonstrations," said one spokeswoman for "Taking Wall Street", Mark Bray.
Bray clarified that "indignant" response to the eviction of the camp, which was commissioned by the mayor of New York, Michael Bloomberg himself, reached the court, where Judge Lucy Billings issued an order forcibly eviction and allowed demonstrators to continue to camp here.
In addition, "indignancies" occupied another park located at the intersection of central Canal Street and Sixth Avenue, near the entrance to the Holland Tunnel, which connects New York and New Jersey, and the parish land in Trinity Church.
For at least an an hour they managed to take the place, but then the police came and deprived some demonstrators. Bray did not know the exact number, but stated that he had seen the handcuffs deployed even to some reporters.
The organization assures that several Hundred people were seated on the spot. A delegation of religious officials also came to express their support and also to try to negotiate with the police.
In the meantime Zuccotti Square, which is a private property, remains closed. A court hearing, which should take place just on Tuesday, is awaited, and during which the legality of morning evacuation should be analyzed.
Some of the relocated demonstrators even distributed and handed out copies of the injunction and even gave them to members who were patrolling the square, provoking "some small clashes," said Bray, condemning the fact that some police officers beat the demonstrators.
Injunction prohibits authorities from evict members of the "Ocupa Wall Street" movement or to use "laws that have been published after embarking, or ban manifesters from accessing the park with shops or other places they had previously exploited".
Mayor of Bloomberg told the press that due to this injunction, the city suspended the re-opening of public space and warned the demonstrators that local laws did not allow them to return with tents and smuggling.
The highest representative of the city personally obstructed Zuccotti Square in the morning eviction, an operation in which 200 people were detained and which was approached because the demonstrators "violated the law".
Bloomberg explained that he commanded the evacuation of the square because it became "a place where people didn't go to protest, but to violate laws and in some cases hurt others" because "some shops received threatening letters" and "neighbors were worried about the quality of their lives."
“Eviction was appallominent in the morning and shows that Bloomberg is trying to maintain financial interests at the expense of the U.S. working-off people,” a spokeswoman for the movement accused the mayor of “imbursing freedom of expulsion.”
Representatives of the "Occupa Wall Street" movement also said they were planning to organize "great actions" on Thursday in cooperation with community and trade union organizations to commemorate the two months after which the protests have lasted. These began on September 17.
