Japan Returns to Life After Devastating Earthquake
GDP rises by 1.5% due to household consumption, ending with a period of falls, which began in March
The Japanese economy has been paralyzed since March of last year, now it is recovering from a devastating earthquake that has escalated into a nuclear-critical crusade, the worst catastrophe that the country has faced since World War 2.
Tokyo's government's first estimates have quantified the material losses at $300 000 million, a blow that heals well despite a difficult global situation.
After three-quare-creaments, the value of GDP between July-September came into such a position that it was possible with a definite effect to rotate: GDP grew by 1.5% compared to the previous quarter and by 6% per annum.
The results of the second quarter - for the Japanese tax year begins in March 2012 - moreover, they assume the highest growth rate compared to January-March 2010.
Resurgence can be partially explained by the purchase of machines that are necessary to rebuild areas destroyed by quarreling and subsequent tsunami.
Entrepreneurs increased their investments by 1.1%, spending will fall 0.9% compared to January-March.
Nevertheless, the true awakening of the Japanese economy stems exclusively from an impetus, which forces us to use the crisis to come out of reinforcements, or to rebuild the infrastructure.
Japan has waithered, mainly due to the development of domestic consumption, which currently accounts for 60% of Japan's GDP.
