Enrolment of the golden rule on a balanced budget into the constitution
The Government of the Grand Coalition of the Social-DEmocrat and the Christian Democrats, which is in power in Austria, decided on Tuesday to enumerate the "golden rule" of budgetary policy into the constitution in order to reduce the public deficit and thus escalated the eventual downgrade of Austria by rating agencies (AAA).
It was the Social Democratic Chancellor Werner Faymann himself who announced this decision at the conclusion of a deliberation of the ministers in a situation where the Austrian public debt, with its 74.6% high above the criterion of no more than 60% established by the Maastricht agreement, but much lower than the debts of countries such as Italy, Greece or Spain.
“Surkey’s solvency had been reduced by just one single degree from AAA to AA+, we’d have had to pay three billion euros more each year,” argued multi-Chancellor and Foreign Minister Michael Spindelegger.
Austria thus follows the request of a eurozone summit held in Brussels on 26 October 2011, where the heads of state and governments strongly asked that by the end of 2012 the reduction of the public deficit and achieve the balance of public accounts be written into the constitution or the same value.
The instrument adopted by the Austrian government is approaching the "German model", Germany was the first European country to accept such a "rule".
Until 2017, the public structural deficit will have to fall by 0.75% of gross domestic product (GDP every year, and from 2017, the public structural deficit of the federal state will not be allowed to exceed 0.35% of GDP every year, while Länder as well as municipalities will have to report a balanced budget.
The aim is to bring the public debt below the criterion of 60% set by Maastricht in 2020/21.
