Energetic policy:
Facts have to triumph over dogmas
If there is a topic that can not be grasped on the basis of short-term deliberations or under emotions, it is an energetic policy.
Disposibility and the price of electricity have a direct impact on the purchasing power and the competitiveness of enterprises.
It's natural for an election meeting to be an opportunity to think about such deposits and a place that occupies energy from the nucleus.
The debate will only be successful if all the economic, social and environmental consequences of the proposed possibilities are explored.
The French must base their position on objective facts and rational data.
So far there are 7 billion human beings on the planet, but demographic development will provoke a doubling of electricity demand by 2050 if we don’t want to deprivoid an important part of the world’s population.
At the same time it will be necessary to face a shortage of fossil resources in order to continue to generate electricity, while avoiding the need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions in order to combat climate change, which is currently disappearing from ecological debate.
Fukushima Accident does not affect any of these parameters.
That’s precisely why Germany itself remains in its decision to reject nuclear energy.
However, this is not the case of Belgium that subordinatelizes its decision on the need to find some other source capable of subdued by itself, nor Switzerland, which it considers to be the right to build only last-generation power plants.
Conversely, the United Kingdom, Finland, Poland, the Czech Republic, the Netherlands, Sweden, South Africa, China, India, Brazil to quote at least some countries continue with determination in our projects.
After Fukushima, the United States has decided to expand its park with one unit by restoring the construction of a power plant interrupted following the accident of the Three Mile Island power plant.
If a German model based on renewable energies is sometimes presented as an example, the reality contradicts it.
The election of Berlin will be reflected in the increase in the price of electricity, the increased energy dependency on gas imported especially from Russia, a supple increase in CO2 emissions induced by the construction of natural gas and coal-fired power plants.
We will feel all these unpleasant effects within a few months.
The Danish case deserves us to think about it.
The European wind farm champion, of which 30% of its electricity, is also one of the world's largest consumers of coal and gas due to non-scheduling wind, which makes CO2 emissions and Danish electricity prices 65% higher than the European average.
Conversely, due to the immutability of our energy policy, our neighbors will pay an electricity bill of 40% higher than the payment of French households.
While in the globalized economy the cost of energy represents a key competitive factor and a guarantee of keeping industry in the national territory, French businesses enjoy the advantages of the cheapest electricity in Europe.
For this reason, any significant reduction in the share of nuclear energy would provoke a strong increase in the price of electricity, which would make an illusory beautiful declaration of re-industrialization of our country and the defence of purchasing power.
A prize that a great majority of French people refuse to pay, just as they fundamentally don’t stand up against this energy, as public opinion polls show.
Recacall that our country's energy policy has made it possible to tribute a sector with 125,000 direct jobs and 410,000 consequent jobs.
A high-trafficult-to-efficient sector is dragged into international markets by a large number of SME-shaTICAL OFFICIABLIC INVALIisation, which cannot be transported abroad.
If France had decided to leave the core, it would sacrrevigate a large number of such small and medium-sized enterprises and would lose 6-bbillion euros per annum that the country receives for exporting nuclear installations and services.
Which owner of the power plant would order a nuclear reactor designed to work for 60 years, while the bankruptcy of its manufacturer would be planned?
It must be admitted that there is no comprehensible source for generating energy when dealing with an energy equation.
The development of low-rise CO2, nuclear installations and renewable energy installations carried out by France's AREVA industrial group are indicative of the complementarity of energy sources.
In the case of nuclear energy, an absolute need to maintain the highest level of safety.
In France we have the State Office for Nuclear Safety, which performs uncompromising and transparent oversight of existing nuclear power plants as well as the construction of a new power plant in Flamanville.
Our teams, whose professionalism is recognized, have the idea of safety in the blood.
Fifty-degreases has recognized the nuclear industry three serious accidents.
The only one in the Three Mile Island took place under conditions of normal operation and has remained free of human casualties and consequences for the environment.
In Chernobyl, the drama caused reactor concepts and inexcusable human errors.
In Fukushima, some seem to forget that the crash was the result of two natural catastrophes of an unprecedented extent that caused the deaths of tens of thousands of people.
The nuclear industry will take the lesson from this accident, just as it has done for the two previous ones.
The fruit of this step and cooperation between the French and German authorities dealing with nuclear safety, operators and constructors is that an EPR-type reactor represents a concept capable of resisting such phenomena.
While all bidders wishing to build a nuclear power plant devise it, halting its construction at Flamanville would be an excellent gift offered to AREVA and EDF's competitors and a easing of the path to seizing a leading stance in nuclear development.
Necessary and it is legitimate to find the best energy solution for our country, but it would be irresponsible to allow emotions, dogma and machination to dominate the debate so decisive for the economic, social and financial situation of our country.
Is Immigration a burden or a chance for an economy?
Immigration policy carried out under Nicolas Sarkozy has been criticized from different angles - overbraivation of the police, the courts and the administration subordinated to the policy, the compatisbility of this policy with the "country of human rights", which France boasts.
Recently it was administrative harassment, the object of which were foreign students, which became topical when the Minister of the Interior (see Libération dated May 23) stated that France "does not need foreign talents, bricklayers and waitresses".
But so far it has rarely been disassembled at an angle of the economy.
It has to be said that in this area there is quite a broad consensus between the left and the right.
On the tone on the tone in 1976, Jacques Chirac stated with his statement that "900000 unemployers should not be a problem in a country where 2 million immigrant workers are located" and Michel Rocard on the left when he said in 1990 that France "could not accept all the misery of the world".
The only difference is the degree of generosity
In 2005, the fear of invading hordes of Polish plumbers on the French territory was shared both on the right and on the to the left.
Immigrants are seen in both camps as a burden on the economy and French society.
The only difference lies in the degree of generosity, which we begin to provide to immigrants.
In its program, François Hollande confines itself to banalities, stating that joint developments will make it possible to solve our “migration problem”.
This idea of Immigration as a burden and the problem may apply in elections, but economically it is very expensive.
As Ian Goldin and Geoffrey's economists and Geoffrey Cameron reminismuggate in their recently published work synwithen the latest knowledge of immigration (Exceptional People: How Migration Shaped Our World and Will Define Our Future in May 2011, Princeton University Press, 352 pp.): there is a broad consensus among specialists on the positive impact of migratory movements on economic growth, wages, and employment in countries that accept immigrants.
Immigration rescinding cripples growth and harm employment.
Concerns about the migrant-driven impacts are based on the idea that these people can replace French workers, especially those less skilled, and create pressure to reduce their wages.
But the experience shows that, in fact, Immigrants are much more supplements than substitutes for French employees by doing work in sectors with staff shortages.
Fewer skilled immigrants in sectors that don't attract French employees, and the most skilled in dynamic sectors in which e-education doesn't supply.
Positive impact on wages
Just as the surgeon will have difficulty working in a country where there is a shortage of anaesthesiologists, these additions between the French and the migrants cause the arrivals of immigrants having a positive effect on the wages and employment of French workers.
Giovanni Peri thus calculated that the growth of migration movements by 1% will cause a long-term increase in real wages between 0.6 and 0.9%.
At the same time, it doesn’t take into account the fact that Immigration’s diversion contributes to the formation of ideas and economic growth (a high proportion of naturalized immigrants among U.S. Nobel laureates; Google, Intel, Paypal, eBay and Yahoo were founded by immigrants).
Migrants in European countries are net contributors of social systems on an average of 1% of the total budget.
For example, the World Occupational Organization estimates that in Germany an immigrant will bring a €15000 €15000 into public budgets throughout its life.
Nicolas Sarkozy expressed at the beginning of his wish at the beginning of his wish to go for the missing percentage of growth "with bared teeth".
Mentioned teeth used to discouraging immigrants had the exact opposite effect.
Europe has inspired a deliberation, it is necessary to think about European democracy
Democracy is a scarce and very fragrant commodity.
The European Convincial Convincial Continent should remember, even though it was invented in Athens two thousand years a year ago, during the 20th century the Tragedies of World Wars, Totalitarian Regimes, Holocaust, Gulags, but also Franca in Spain, Salazar in Portugal and colonels in Greece.
Some people might have believed that in order for democracy to definitively triumph over its enemies, it would be enough to make a choice and carry it out in the most direct way possible.
However, it is known from Napoleon that it was an ease of thought.
Universal suffrional suffrage, although a necessary precondition for democracies, is not a sufficient condition and it can become an "instrument of oppression" as the republican philosopher Etienne Vacherot once said.
No, democracy requires much more: public space, public spirit, values, organization of separation and control of power, education, enlightenment, social and economic solidarity, judiciary.
The crisis of Europe we are going through is an economic crisis, but it is just like that and from the beginning of the crises of politics and democracy.
Confessing the mistake of the Greek so-no referendum will bear the value of a double revelation in this regard: revealing the need for a great return of political power, but also the revelation of a disturbing sense of its impossibility, its inappropriateness.
This ambivalence must be thought out in order to be overcome.
It is true that the front lines of democratic struggle have relocated today, and that many of the Republican fighters are no longer shooting in the right direction.
We cannot get anything if we require a number of national referendums organized according to necessity and disorganized.
Bianko exposed to a European absolutist monarch or a jump with tied feet into an emptiness of the nationalist depths: what a choice for a citizen!
No, we don't want guillotine knives, we don't want a common, full-bodied, peaceful and unstoppable pursuit of choice and democracy, this time at European level.
There are no crises in Greece, Italy, Spain, Portugal - we wouldn't even know where to halt the enumerative - because the crisis is in Europe.
It's the whole of a whole Europe bowing before the dictates of markets and credit rating agencies, which suffers from its incompetence and its lack of solidarity.
It's the whole of Europe that is humiliated on an international stage.
We get discouraged in the transmission of sovereignty to Europe - but it is the whole of Europe, which is slowly losing its sovereignty, and with it each of its peoples.
It is urgently necessary to return the governance of the European project of democratic sovereignty.
For only jointly will Europeans be able to reduce the burdens of their debts, to get rid of the governments of the markets and to prepare the future by investing.
The east of the crisis will be forsaken decisions at the European level, which will not be accepted without complete democratic legitimacy, otherwise it would lead us to a scoundrel.
Having fought for a long time against this proposal of the Socialists and Jacques Delors, everyone today strongly demands the European economic government.
Oh, so be it.
But to think this without a stronger political and democratic integration, and experienced as such citizens, there would be a new folly and a new bleated alley.
We have to take on a new phase of European construction.
In order to create a republic in France, it was necessary to "being" republicans.
Revolution in interests could not be enough for this.
This lesson continues to apply.
We have to build a Europe that will be more economically integrated.
This can only be carried out by constructing Europe more political-democratic and truly European democratic sovereignty.
Let's become Europeans to build a Europe.
