Nord Stream, a new harbor of Russian gas to Europe
The Nord Stream long-distance gas pipeline connecting Eastern Europe directly to Russia and running along the Baltic Sea has begun to operate.
It must supply gas equal to the equivalent of 30 million households.
Gazprom, the Russian gas giant, wanted to properly celebrate the event.
More than 500 guests, among them 4 heads of states and governments, took part in the inauguration of the Nord Stream, a new gas pipeline under the sea that will directly connect Russia and Germany under the sea.
Separately German Chancellor Angela Merkel, Russian President Dmitry Medvedev, French Prime Minister François Fillon and his Dutch colleague Mark Rutte have been turned jointly through the control wheel of the Russian gas control input into the Western European network.
27.7.5 billion additional cubic meters of Russian gas with the first 1,224-kilometre-supplied St. Petersburg-Pomeranian-Pomeranian-Pomeřany pipeline into Western Europe every year.
The second pipeline, which has to be completed at the end of 2012, will make it possible to supply a total of 55 billion cubic meters of gas, which is an amount equal to the entire French consumption.
Recognizes the possibility to build a third pipeline pipeline on the same route.
For Gazprom, the timely completion of this huge construction has the character of a victory.
Poland and the Baltic countries strongly objected to the project.
In fact, Nord Stream will make it possible to supply gas directly to Germany without being run by a third country (Poland or Ukraine).
At the same time, these two countries lose a little bit of power: the possibility of pushing Russia with the threat of taking access to Western-European markets is becoming more difficult for them.
This was most strongly expressed by Warsaw when it did not hesitate to compare Nord Stream to the "New German-Soviet Pact", citing an alliance between Hitler and Stalin during the occupation of Poland in 1939.
It's supposed to say it's politically sensitive.
Western Europe, with its support for this Russian project, gained greater certainty of its purchases.
But it's at the cost of weakening Europe's energy, because the project has created cracks among Europeans from the East and the West.
Russia managed barely 5 years ago to assert its vision for a direct alliance between Russia and the wealthy Western states with high gas consumption whose deposits are coming to an end.
"This gas pipeline is a component of our partnership with Russia, a new artery that connects us organically," François Fillon confirmed at the opening of this gas pipeline, demonstrating at what point large gas contracts are both political and economic.
All of this is explained by the broad smiles of Gazprom’s leading representatives present in large numbers in Lubmin, a gas supply site.
The construction of this gas pipeline is also an important technical act.
An undersemi-oil pulp is made of steel with a concrete cladding in order to be protected from the anchors of seagoing vessels.
It rests at the bottom of the sea at an average depth of 200 m.
During its construction it had to be evacuated minefields dating back to World War 2.
“Nearly a hundred had to be eliminated or devastated due to the safety,” says Jens Müller, a spokesman for Nord Stream.
It was also necessary to increase measures to protect fauna and flora.
The pipe itself is a long metal snake weighing like 242 Eiffel Towers, which is not interrupted anywhere on its entire length by valves or maintenance stations.
One control platform was supposed to be built near Sweden, but the project manager dismissed it when the Swedes were little greedy to see a Russian state-of-the-art state-sponsive state-of-the-art settling permanently off their coast.
"In the event of a glitch, we will halt the passage of gas in less than 1 minute," Jens Müller clarifies.
In the case of leaks, the gas contained in the gas pipeline escapes into the air.
But this case is considered very probable by the builder, since welds have been designed in such a way that the boulia has arisen once every 100 000 years.
Inspecting the condition of the gas pipeline from the outside is carried out by an undersea-cavity-dragged ship, while the interior of the gas pipeline is inspected by a device circulating inside.
This cigar-shaped device with a diameter of 1 meter is inserted into the interior of the pipe and is pressurized by gas.
At the finish for the whole three-day journey at a speed of 3 m/s it will provide accurate information about possible deformations of the metal parts.
The long-distance gas pipeline has been designed to work for fifty years without the slightest repair.
Circulation of the gas is simply ensured by pressure.
The value of this pressure is 200 bar at the start and 100 bar at the finish.
For the implementation of this project, five companies have joined forces.
Gazprom, which holds a 51% stake, remains the position.
It was joined by German companies E.ON and BASF (15.5% each), as well as Dutch company Gasunie and the French group GDF Suez (9% each).
These five companies created a joint-committed enterprise called Nord Stream tasked with the construction and operation of the gas pipeline.
The company is headquartered in Zoug in Switzerland, where taxation is extremely advantageous.
Gazprom subsequently leased the company for a five-year contract, which can be re-renewed, all its capacities for the transport of gas.
“Gazprom will make a profit out of the total capacity, whatever the amount of gas actually transported,” Müller said.
Financing the construction of the gas pipeline was ensured from a 30% shareholder and a 70% banking loan.
The total cost of the works should reach 7.4 billion euros for both pipelines.
For GDF Suez, investment costs have risen to $240 million.
“Musinary shareholders are sure that their costs will be reimbursed, because only Gazprom takes the risk.
The gas pipeline rises from the Baltic Sea in Lubmin on the sandy coast with pine forests.
Vents 4.5 meters high form the outlet of the gas gate.
The sensors measure the pressure, temperature and other characteristics of the incoming gas before being transported to German gas pipelines.
Since last Tuesday, this terminal has been receiving one million cubic meters of Russian gas every hour.
If the full capacity is reached, it will be 3 million per hour and 6 million in 2012.
