Moleskine profites from its success and this cultural aura and diversifies its products: bags, diaries, pencils, laptop cases ... The brand expands to a variety of carriers.
Suspension awaken: Modo Modo Modo was bailed up by investment funds SG Capital Europe, today Syntegra Capital, writing a new page in Moleskine history.
Why is there such a high proportion of unemployed handicapped workers?
Around 19% of handicapped people are looking for a job.
Today it opens the 15th week for the employment of the handicapped.
Despite the voluntaristic public policy introduced in 2005, a percentage of unemployed disembodied people is twice as high as the average, i.e. 19% from 9% for the entire population.
Is it daunting?
"No, underscored by Éric Blanchet, Director-in-general of the Adapt Association, which is organizing the Week for the Employing of Disabled."
"Proceduties achieved in 15 years are noteworthy."
"At that time the percentage of unemployment was not twice, but three times higher."
"Nevertheless, there's still a lot left to do."
It must be said that for unemployed handicapped persons, there remain numerous obstacles prior to their entry into work.
These are the elderly: 37% have more than 50 years (17% on average).
They have a low graduation graduation graduation: 80% do not have gradgraduation, and out of the 2.3 million university students, only 10 000 are handicapped.
Finally, long-term unemployment is very widespread: 53% for handicapped versus 38% on average.
In short, such are the criteria that particularly complicate the return to employment and which represent the "core of the problem", according to Christian Grapin, Director-general of Tremplin, which was founded in 1992, to help businesses hire handicapped workers.
Too much when it comes to objective brakes.
Unspeaking, there are ways of thinking, which certainly develop, but too slowly.
A study carried out in April by IMS-Entreprendre pour la Cité found that interviewed managers have a "weak level of knowledge about handicapped persons" with an exaggerated notion of persons in a mobile armchair and mental deficiencies".
As a result, private enterprises with more than 20 employees are still far away from achieving the objective of employing 6% of disabled workers set out in the Act of 1987.
The last known numbers, which come from 2008, indicate a state equal to 2.6%.
“We are now around 3%,” says Pierre Blanc, CEO of Agefiph in charge of levying fees from businesses that don’t obliute the law.
Evidence is the number of "finited" enterprises, as well as the amounts paid, which decreased between 2007 and 2011 from 59,000 guilty with undertakings to 46,000 (and from 606 to 480 million euros).
"The 2005 law, which increased contributions, created strong pressure on businesses, explains Pierre Blanc."
"But this improvement also demonstrates real awareness, especially in large enterprises."
According to the Ministry of Labour, only 59% of enterprises with 20 to 49 employees employ handicapped workers compared to 82% of enterprises with 100 to 199 employees.
